select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "6f8aa2d4-be06-40f7-861b-117a34bb1a92", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "John Drake (07:03): Yeah, we worked with Visit Idaho for a long, long time. We learned from that we have to, we learned a few things. First of all, we have to define the problem really well. And the problem was visit Idaho is largely outspent. I mean, a lot of states and areas have a lot more revenue to put for tourism than Idaho does. And so it's like recognizing that from the outset that were significantly outspent. And then what we did is we really tried to hone it. We really honed the media target down. So we said, okay, who are we really trying to, we've got this little pool of money, so who do we focus it on? And so what we decided on, we coined this target, which has been really helpful, which was we wanted to spend, we wanted to mostly spend to get families with kids. Lemme see if I can do this family with kids under the age of 18 living at home who have an affinity for the outdoor adventure and live within an uncomplicated day's journey to Idaho.\n\nSpeaker 3 (08:07): Wow,\n\nJohn Drake (08:07): That's\n\nSpeaker 3 (08:08): Specific.", "token_estimate": 263, "speakers": ["John Drake", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "07:03", "end_timestamp": "08:08", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "9b125665-e19f-4fd2-b67b-a1af57d42542", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "John Drake (08:09): Yeah, we really wanted to do that and we really wanted to do that really, really well. And there's some words in there that would be defined so that the team knew what they meant, but that was really who we tried to get to. And we said, if we can just get those people to be aware of what happens when you travel to Idaho, that's what a lot of the paid media needs to do. So know about it. And then once we know a little bit about you, you visited the site, you've done something, then we can say, oh, you're interested in this thing, so let's let you know what can happen there. So we learned to be really focused there and that was good. And you take learnings. That's one of the great things about this industry, I think, is you take learnings from one industry and you can see where that sort of learning can apply to others. And so it was good. And across the mid-market it's fun because you work on different things. So we have industries that we do a lot of and we like to think that we do an okay job there, but we also, I think what clients come to us for is we really try to understand their brand and do it well. And they like that we have a little bit of experience maybe in the category, but we're not a dedicated agency in one said category, and we find that our clients like that.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:28): Yeah. Who are some other examples of your clients?\n\nJohn Drake (09:32): We have worked with CBH Homes for many, many years. They're a fantastic organization. Home builder based out of Boise, (09:41): Idaho Central Credit Union, which is a great organization doing all the right things. We worked with 44 North Vodka for many, many years, many years. We work with Newland Communities, which is a master plant community, which is a great organization. And then we do a lot of work with Blue Diamond almonds. We work with Boeing a little bit. And then we have a lot of clients where we just won a best of show award for a company called Bornstein Seafood up by where you're based, which fantastic company. So we span across several areas and really just look for great clients to work with.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["John Drake", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:09", "end_timestamp": "09:32", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "def3abef-61c2-4f2c-8524-bc60897373a3", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:25): One of the things you mentioned in our phone call earlier last week or the week before, you were talking about why you guys focus in on the mid-size market and it's very intentional why you guys choose to put your energy in business development towards that segment. Why is that your focus and why don't you look for the larger brands to work with\n\nJohn Drake (10:51): In the mid-market? We find that there is an awesome sort of hunger people. We find that clients who work there in the midmarket, they're really still a mid-market company, can still be a big company, but it's not a gargantuan company. And we find that that environment really creates, people who work there still have a connection to the CEO, they know that person often or they see that person walking around and that size of organization you get into. People really try to, they really understand what the company often, what the company's trying to do, what the brand is all about. They all treat the brand very seriously. They also really enjoy a personal relationship. They're very collaborative, we find and very pleasant to do business with. And it's just a great, if you can summarize an entire size of doing business, which is of course almost impossible to do, but to be very general overall, it's great because you get really good people and they've generated enough revenue to do some things with advertising and branding, which is interesting.\n\nSpeaker 3 (12:10): And\n\nJohn Drake (12:11): They appreciate the fact that we are a little bit more off the grid than a lot of agencies. I mean, we're not in New York or la and that is we find sometimes welcome to buy clients. They like that. A lot of good agencies obviously in those markets, but we're not, and we think that is an advantage for a way of doing business and a way of thinking.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:38): What can you do when your clients are maybe a little, are more okay with that creative freedom and passing it off to you guys? What does that allow you to do?", "token_estimate": 507, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Drake", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "10:25", "end_timestamp": "12:38", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "0c8dd657-2f69-40ef-8fbd-d6c62405258d", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "John Drake (12:49): It allows us to take a few more risks. It allows us to be really collaborative. It allows us to bring people in for a meeting for a surprise, and everybody's cool with it. It's like, Hey, come join us over here. Let's do it. No, we don't have to answer to anybody. There's no big sort of holding company or anything. We don't have to say, can we talk to this team over here and bring them in and have a bunch of meetings about that? We just kind of do it. And that's really good for creativity. That's really good to not have those walls and we can move a little more nimbly. Is that a word more nimbly\n\nCole Heilborn (13:30): Maybe? I think so. If it isn't, it\n\nJohn Drake (13:32): Is now.\n\nSpeaker 3 (13:33): There you go.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:35): Yeah, no, that's really well said. And when you're looking towards the future of advertising and marketing and the agency, what role does creativity play in the future? Do you think?\n\nJohn Drake (13:55): There will always be a need for organizations to be presented with new ideas and to think about things a little bit differently or to have creative people just look at something a little bit differently. (14:18): Malcolm Gladwell, wasn't it, who said that? You got to know what you're solving. The puzzle or the riddle always kind of like that where it's like the puzzle is like you're missing a piece and we need to go find that piece and we do research or we do something to get it. Then there's the riddle, which is you've got all the pieces. For the most part, they're there. It's the vantage point of how you look at it. I think that there will always be a need for organizations to go out and realize they're solving a puzzle or they're solving a riddle and they need creative people to look at it and say, we've gotten it this far. We think it's about these things. What do you think? I just can't see that ever changing with the future. And that's I think where creative organizations can really help companies be as good as they can be. Do", "token_estimate": 506, "speakers": ["John Drake", "Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "12:49", "end_timestamp": "13:55", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "dddd7660-50f2-4479-8ebe-777dd8525151", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:08): You think that creativity is enough to, and I know this is really broad because you say creativity and there's a million ways you could define it or look at it, but do you think creativity is what is needed to separate a business in today's market or in the future? Is that all it takes or does it take more than that?\n\nJohn Drake (15:29): It takes brilliant execution. If you're a company who can give people a good value, however you define value, and if you can execute it brilliantly so that they have a great experience, that's first and foremost what it takes. We think that we have this, we talk about consumers in three ways. We think that at Dre Cooper, what we say is that the consumer, however you define it, people really, they kind of want three things. They expect to easily find what they want and get what they need wherever they are. That should be, they want that. They dislike being bothered by things that don't help them, and they want to win. They want to win. And however you define winning, if that's, they want to save money or they want to be acknowledged for something, or they want to be delighted with something they want to win. And so they want those three things. And so if an organization can do that at its core, bring that out into the world, and then we can help them see that we can help articulate it or we can help get it noticed or be distinctive about it. (16:40): That's where we can be a helpful partner.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:43): And is that where you see differentiation coming into play between brands?\n\nJohn Drake (16:49): I do. At least from our line of work. I think that it starts at the beginning. Can an organization be, can they do something that's valuable? And then are they saying it in a way that people understand it and believe it and get excited about it? And yeah, I think that's how a lot of companies can stand out and be different from each other.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:13): Yeah, yeah. So tell me a little bit, we're going to change gears a little bit. Tell me a little bit about your background. You've got a ton of experience in the ad world. Give us the highlights.", "token_estimate": 538, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Drake"], "start_timestamp": "15:08", "end_timestamp": "17:13", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "095b7075-bfb9-4c3a-817e-30d9247e5cc7", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "John Drake (17:27): Yes. Well, I started reading advertising. I was kind of an advertising nerdy kid, and I started reading advertising age when I was like 15 or 16 years old, and I for some reason really liked it. And I was one of those kids who put ads up. I would rip out ads out of magazines and tape them up my room, and I always liked that. And so I got my degree in advertising and I moved to LA after I graduated college and worked at Daily and Associates for a while, worked at Deutsche for many, many years and Wong Duty. And I've done account management, I've done strategy, and I've done business development all in advertising agencies. I've only worked in that agencies. (18:12): And I got really lucky because I worked at Deutsche LA at a time when I was the 19th employee there. While I was there, it grew to four or 500 people, and it was the first time that an agency based on the East coast had ever established a really successful office on the west coast to such a degree. And it was a time they won, and Deutsche won advertising agency the year, five years in a row from Adweek and Adage, and it was just this amazing run. And so I got to work with you. You take hundreds of people and you put them quickly into an organization, you're going to bump into amazing people. And I got really, really lucky because you meet and work with people in that environment, not just because it was big, but because of the nature of what was happening there. I just got lucky. It would've been like Kristen Porter in our industry a few years later, and those people went on and did really interesting things. And so I got lucky and that really helped me. And then from there, I joined Drake Cooper and had been with the agency since 2007.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:15): Sounds like what you're saying is you were kind of in the right time at the right place. Would you say for people who are fresh out of college or wanting to get into the ad world, it sounds like you're saying is it's really all about the people that you meet and the people that you know. Is that one of the things you've taken away from your time in the ad world?", "token_estimate": 534, "speakers": ["John Drake", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:27", "end_timestamp": "19:15", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "b7fe1d25-9962-4142-abe0-37e952e41bd5", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "John Drake (19:33): For sure. Yeah. I think that advertising gets, it's a great industry, a cool industry. It's the ability to be in business, if you will, but also at the creative end of that. It's really interesting work and you can do it from anywhere. That's what's really neat about it. I mean, almost anywhere. I mean, you have to go to a really small city, really small town to not have a need for some sort of advertising or branding or marketing. And it's really cool because you can live in the city that you want, the place that you want and do this. It's great. I encourage anybody who, and it picks up a lot of interesting people because it picks up the people who don't always know where they fit.\n\nSpeaker 3 (20:23): They\n\nJohn Drake (20:23): Like to be artistic, they like creativity, they like people often and they kind of like business, but they may not like myself. Business that doesn't come naturally. It's not like, but they like it. And so advertising exists to capture all these really interesting people where you can use your art or use your writing or use your strategy or use your people skills or use your affinity for the media or your ability to think about data and get insights. It captures all these really interesting people and puts them together. I think it's a really great thing to do.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:59): Yeah. Do you have any examples or, I don't know, memories of moments in the industry where you had, I don't know, you felt the most joy or you felt the most contentment with what you were doing?\n\nJohn Drake (21:12): They all come back to when the team works really, really hard, you think of a really interesting idea. You think it's good you, and then you show it to a client and they say, that's good, let's do that. And that moment is so gratifying because you have a lot of people who work so hard and then somebody else looks at 'em and says, yeah, that's really great. We should do that. And just take that every time that happens on small things and big things, it feels great. And then when you see your workout in the world, that's kind of cool. That's neat.", "token_estimate": 530, "speakers": ["John Drake", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:33", "end_timestamp": "21:12", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "45a47652-e8c5-42f3-af8e-a65b61795635", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (21:50): Yeah. Do you think that there's something unique about the culture of an ad agency that allows just all these various types of people with different backgrounds and different personalities to all work well together? Is there something unique about the ad culture versus a different business culture that allows that to work?\n\nJohn Drake (22:09): I can't speak to other businesses specifically because I've only worked in ad agencies. What I can say though, from working at big agencies and small agencies is that overall people are pretty cool. They are overall pretty nice. They're overall well to work with. They're overall interesting. They do things outside of work, if you will, that are fun to hear about, and you put those people together and that can make for a really neat environment. I have worked some people, and you do get, sometimes it's not always unicorns and rainbows, but overall I think that it's a neat industry and I think that people over the years, different places you worked with people, it's like, oh, that wasn't necessarily great. But in general, I would say that as a generality, people are cool.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:20): Alright, so one of the last things I want to talk about with our time today is Drake Cooper's background because kind of got a personal connection to the agency you're with now.", "token_estimate": 338, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Drake"], "start_timestamp": "21:50", "end_timestamp": "23:20", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "95546398-e9a3-4633-88b7-23bf872c01dc", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "John Drake (23:31): Bill Drake started it and Bill Drake would be my father, and he started it in 1978. And a lot of entrepreneurs and people who start agencies, they looked at the industry and said, think I can do this a little bit differently. And he went and did that. And so he built it very quickly to be the largest agency in Idaho. Within I think five or six years it became that. (23:54): And it's been going ever since. And it's always this, it's always wanted to be, Cooper is a brand, it's always wanted to be a highly creative organization where the work is really, really important and we want to have clients for a long time. We think that we get the really good work. We get sometimes the best work when we know somebody for a really long time. So you want to have an environment where we really love our clients, we want them to be happy and successful. And so it's blending those two things together, which can happen. Sometimes you hear that can't happen, but it can. And we really tried to do that. And so I joined in 2007 after working elsewhere to work there. And Jamie Cooper's the CEO and president, and it's been fabulous working together with him.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:50): Was there ever a time when you and your dad crossed paths in your career?", "token_estimate": 318, "speakers": ["John Drake", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:31", "end_timestamp": "24:50", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "f2e4a2ce-d5cb-4b26-8100-13ec658827a7", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "John Drake (24:56): Yes, there was. And it's funny because in 2007 I was like, oh, should I do this? And was thinking about it. I thought, okay, there's all sorts of things that you, okay. It was at the time he was the head of the company. And so I was like, do I want to work with my father? We have a great relationship, but it's one of the things you think about and you weigh all these pros and cons. And I thought about it. I thought about it. So I mean, I do strategy. I thought about it, it all, and I had it all figured out. I was like, yes, we're going to do this. It's going to be great. Well, so funny story because, so I think I get there, I arrive in Boise. I'm in my first meeting and he's in the meeting and there's a bunch of people in the meeting. We're sitting at opposite sides of the room, and I have to get his attention. And suddenly I froze because I realized something I'd never thought about before, which was like, what the hell do I call him? Because the moment I say, dad, that throws the meeting into this funky place, (25:55): But the moment I call him Bill by his first name, all of a sudden it's like, well, here's his son, trying not to call him\n\nSpeaker 3 (26:00): Dad.\n\nJohn Drake (26:01): And so I was like, I didn't know what to call him. And so for years I would just get his attention. What do you think? I'd make noise and get him to look at me. But I never really knew what to call him in business. And so for years when he was still at the company, I would never call him by name because it just felt so strange in either scenario. And that's something I didn't really think about until it was occurring for the first time in that meeting in 2007.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:27): And you guys never talked about that out of work or after he retired?\n\nJohn Drake (26:32): No, I don't think so. I think he'll probably hear about that for the first time here if he chooses to listen.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:39): Oh, man. Well, that's pretty funny. But I think that's about all the time we've got today. If people who are listening want to find you or Drake Cooper, where can they go to check out the work You guys are doing?", "token_estimate": 538, "speakers": ["John Drake", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:56", "end_timestamp": "26:39", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "45570ea2-0171-4da2-86f6-0bd8dbf701d1", "episode_id": "e600d296-07ae-4743-8610-17f024eaa9a8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483b", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "John Drake (26:51): Drake cooper.com and we try to be active on social. And yeah, there's a lot of really interesting people there doing really cool stuff. So if you're interested, yeah, check out the website or follow the channels, if you will.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:06): Okay, great. Sounds good. Well, John, thank you so much for being on the show.\n\nJohn Drake (27:10): Thanks, Cole. Really appreciate it, man.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:12): All right. Take care.\n\nJohn Drake (27:13): Bye.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:14): Thank you for listening to this episode of Brand Story. If you enjoyed today's episode, please consider leaving us a review on Apple Podcast or sharing this with a friend or colleague. Tune in next time for another episode.", "token_estimate": 183, "speakers": ["John Drake", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:51", "end_timestamp": "27:14", "podcast_index": 10, "episode_name": "Ep 10: John Drake | A Life in the Ad World | Drake Coope‪r‬", "slug": "ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-10-john-drake-a-life-in-the-ad-world-drake-cooper", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We have this little pool of money, who do we focus it on?\" \"...we have to really define the problem in order to help.\"", "podcast_length": "44:32"}, {"id": "ef6ca6bb-0bc9-4153-895f-9e8c04323e46", "episode_id": "3bec55a4-71e0-40b7-a9a0-00c9d02fb78d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482d", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Unknown: Unknown Cole Today I'm sitting down with Josh Weichhand. He is the Vice President of Global Brand Marketing over at Rumpl. Before this though, Josh, you were at YETI. Before that, you were at Madcap. You're doing some consulting in there. And then if you go back far enough, you were at Chaco way back in the day and you were actually, you're on the show on episode 12. So this is a full circle moment for us. Josh Yeah, about four years later, I think, full circle, starting with the pandemic and now coming back to talk about the results. Cole Well, it's great to see you. You've certainly been around the block and had some neat experiences since we last had you on the show. Funny, I think back to episode 12 and I don't know that anyone could pay me enough money to go back and listen to that conversation, not because of you, because I feel like I don't know if I'd want to listen to myself. I feel like it took me a good 50 episodes to figure out a good workflow and a good process and figure out how do I want to be a host? So thank you for bearing with me through those initial episodes. Josh No, I appreciate it, man. It's a testament to the staying power. I mean, here we are, 160 episodes later, still doing the same thing. Super cool.", "token_estimate": 314, "speakers": [], "start_timestamp": null, "end_timestamp": null, "podcast_index": 12, "episode_name": "Ep 12: Josh Weichhand | Finding and Using your Voice | Chac‪o", "slug": "ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's something about having this one really great pair of sandals that have been with me through it all and then getting to share it with people professionally.\"", "podcast_length": "44:34"}, {"id": "2b8ba251-b6b1-480b-8b91-da43c3325c08", "episode_id": "3bec55a4-71e0-40b7-a9a0-00c9d02fb78d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482d", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole (02:12.75): Still doing the same thing, still having fun, insightful conversations. And speaking of which, we've got a fun one for today. We're going to be talking about inflection points. I don't think it's any secret that our industry is going through change in multiple ways. And Josh, you've got some ideas about inflection points and the changes and things are up in the air at this point. And as you described our industry, like there's kind of a wildfire burning through the industry and it brings a lot of questions about how do we adapt? How do we move forwards? What does this mean for brands today? And so with change comes a lot of questions. And so you've got a bit of the forefront of some of this stuff, it sounds like. And I'm excited to kind of dig in and pick your brain on inflection points in this wildfire metaphor blazing through our industry. Great. Well, thanks. Yeah. Yeah, let's jump right in. I'm curious, why is this something you think about? Let's start there. Can you give us an opening statement about this metaphor that you've been using? Josh Yeah, so I suppose the thesis is inflection points are like forest fires. And by that, I mean they are inherently disruptive and disruptive, but ultimately healthy for the ecosystem. You know, forest fires clear the landscape. They make way for new growth. I think that, you know, in 2024, we're facing kind of the you know, the downside of what was a pandemic bump. And, you know, I'm old enough and I've been doing this long enough that I've seen a couple boom and bust cycles for outdoor just in the last, you know, 15, 20 years. And for somebody who really wanted to make this my life's work and make this a career, you know, I think it really has professionals of a certain age really starting to think about the structural issues and maybe some of the macro conditions that make this sort of thing possible. You know, the last time you and I connected four years ago, the pandemic was just beginning. Chaco had actually converted its factory to make PPE, you know, face masks and protective coverings. And I mean, that's how that's how dire the circumstances were. I mean, we weren't really selling product. Almost all of our dealers had closed their doors. You know, thankfully we had a relatively robust e-commerce operation, but it was very scary and unusual. And I think that the sky was falling until we realized, oh, when there's nothing else to do, people are going outside. And, you know, so began a pretty incredible period of growth, I think, during the pandemic. And I think that the outdoor industry, you know, coupled, coupling that pandemic, consumer behavior and the shift in interest and you know, maybe it was just the obligation of like I have nothing better to do and nowhere else to spend my money plus stimulus. But also, you know, the industry itself had done great work really advocating for outdoor access, getting more people outside. And I think that the culmination of our collective success in winning over the hearts and minds of consumers and also a once in a lifetime event that drove more casual users outside than ever. It led to this situation where I think a lot of brands became a little bit high on their own supply and they started to believe in their success as being this inevitable conclusion. And what you ended up seeing is, you know, over the course of the later pandemic, a lot of brands really starting to creep their scope and really reach into new categories where frankly it wasn't their core focus. It wasn't their core relevance and it was an attempt to grab more consumer market share and mind share. And I think what you're seeing now is, OK, you know, in 2023, we pumped so much inventory into the marketplace. And I'll say that, you know, speaking on behalf of Rumpl as well, I think we're in a similar situation. Like we put a lot of product out into the marketplace and frankly, consumers just aren't metabolizing it at the at the rate that they were during the pandemic and I think that, you know, inevitably it leads to skew rationalization and cost cutting and all the measures that require brands to get serious about like what is our actual focus and what do we want this next stage to look like. And so I think you're going to start to see brands really consolidate down their line plan. I think you're going to see people pivot back to a focus on a core consumer and maybe a core use case. (06:47.406) Brands that were maybe trying to reach into new categories or expand their aperture to reach consumers that maybe their core product wasn't necessarily designed to reach. I think you're going to see some corrective action on that front. Ultimately, it's just a really interesting time. One of the reasons I want to have this conversation is because I think that there are some really interesting learnings for Rumpll that can be extrapolated and applied to the entire industry. I would love to see us come out of this period. You know with with a little bit of a mindset of like not necessarily how can you avoid the forest fire? But how can you adapt to it so that you aren't necessarily you know all consumed? By by these macro conditions and these circumstances. I think there's a lot of things that you know in hindsight were avoidable and That's the stuff. I'm really interested in talking about. Cole So by the time folks this episode is out There's an episode out that I did with Matt Powell. He's an analyst and a researcher and he's been he's been studying the industry for the last 50 years and he and I had a really interesting conversation about this idea, but looking at it from the numbers perspective. And he was saying that, you know, the revenue and the growth of the industry has had over the last couple of years. If we took out the pandemic bump, like we would still be on track for growth, but because we've had this bump in revenue as industry as a whole, because of the pandemic, that's why we're in this decline. It was a short bump and now we're kind of backsliding. But the industry as a whole is still on track for its regular growth percentage. I think he said that was four or five percent, which honestly isn't something that I had heard before. I'd always just assumed like, you know, the sky was falling, but there was no explanation as to why. But he was saying it's really just because we've had this massive increase and now things are adjusting, things are resetting. But that doesn't discount the real world impact that that has is you look around the landscape and you see companies with layoffs, you see people losing their jobs, you see brands questioning, like, what do we look like moving forwards? So it's an interesting time. And I'm curious, are you optimistic? Where does your optimism fall for the industry as a whole as you kind of evaluate where we're at today? I'm inherently an optimistic person.", "token_estimate": 1726, "speakers": ["Cole"], "start_timestamp": "02:12.75", "end_timestamp": "02:12.75", "podcast_index": 12, "episode_name": "Ep 12: Josh Weichhand | Finding and Using your Voice | Chac‪o", "slug": "ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's something about having this one really great pair of sandals that have been with me through it all and then getting to share it with people professionally.\"", "podcast_length": "44:34"}, {"id": "4b0fc7a5-837a-43bd-8ba0-335370250b50", "episode_id": "3bec55a4-71e0-40b7-a9a0-00c9d02fb78d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482d", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Josh (09:10.542): You know, one of the reasons why I'm in this industry is because, you know, I just feel like being connected to the outdoors just makes people come alive and it makes people a better version of themselves and maybe also contextualizes our relevance and our importance in the grand scheme of nature, you know, and I love that. So optimistically, I would say, like, I still absolutely believe in the role of this industry, but I also think we have to be like super clear-eyed around the challenges, you know. And I think that there's both these near-term challenges and then these longer-term challenges, right? And like, the near-term, I think, is stuff everybody's talking about. And it's being discussed ad nauseum, I think, in the industry publications and on LinkedIn and where people from the outdoor space come and have these conversations. But we put way too much inventory into the marketplace, right? You know, to use an example from my time in coffee, one of the things that was really interesting to me about getting into specialty coffee, you know, coming out of the outdoor industry was because the pandemic was in full force, there was this new mode of consumption and purchase, which was subscription or grocery delivery, you know, and there was this question of like, will this become a sticky behavior? You know, will consumers just be like, you know what, I just want my coffee delivered. you know, or I want my groceries delivered every day and I don't even want to have to think about it. And I think that there was a convenience aspect to it that, you know, kicked in in the early days of the pandemic. But quickly what you realized is, you know, people are social beings and they crave the ability to get out and discover and experience new things. And so anything that was going to make their life feel like Groundhog's Day, you know, like it was just being repeated, you know, quickly became old and tired. In the moment, people had an opportunity to break out and get back to normal. I think that they rushed towards those opportunities. And so to get back to the outdoor space, I think what you're seeing is some pretty dramatic shifts in consumer priority and consumer spending patterns. And I would say the things that make me really optimistic about Rumble.", "token_estimate": 576, "speakers": ["Josh"], "start_timestamp": "09:10.542", "end_timestamp": "09:10.542", "podcast_index": 12, "episode_name": "Ep 12: Josh Weichhand | Finding and Using your Voice | Chac‪o", "slug": "ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's something about having this one really great pair of sandals that have been with me through it all and then getting to share it with people professionally.\"", "podcast_length": "44:34"}, {"id": "9c56ed61-08c9-4132-90ce-525ae493eed8", "episode_id": "3bec55a4-71e0-40b7-a9a0-00c9d02fb78d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482d", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Josh (11:27.118): Aside from the incredible brand and the incredible foundation that we have and the strong leadership and all the great things that make this team wonderful, I think is that we are maybe better positioned than a lot of other outdoor brands or certain categories to be able to pivot into the short -term opportunities. And by that, I mean, if you think about the brands that are really going to succeed in this moment, 2024 is going to be a massive travel year you know consumers are going to be spending more on travel and travel experiences than they have, you know, since the previous high of 2019 before the pandemic. And I think that you couple that with what's happening in the housing sector, you know, Gen Z, millennial consumers, maybe reconciling the fact that they're not interested in owning a home. So why save for it? I would rather invest in experiences. So I think that that's a giant opportunity. And then also the boomer generation beginning to retire or to sell off their home that is increased massively in value. That's an opportunity for them to buy the RV or the recreational overlanding vehicle or the custom van to get out on the road. And so a brand like Rumpel, I think, is well positioned to address that particular use case. But then if you think about outdoor brands just how they address the elements. You know, a lot of people are still spending a lot of time outdoors. And I think you would see this quantified in the Outdoor Industry Association's reports on the size of the economy. But the things that are going to be included in it, like the growth categories, aren't necessarily going to be adventure travel, and it's not necessarily going to be mountaineering or alpining or even backcountry skiing. I think it's going to be things like, you know, I spend weekends going to sporting events with my kids, you know, and… travel sports are back and I'm gonna spend all afternoon at a track meet and I need XYZ product to make that experience more comfortable because of the weather, because of the exposure to the cold or the elements, et cetera, et cetera. And so I think that there's sort of this interim period where while we're in a bit of a liminal space as an industry, there are certain brands I think who can sort of index into those sorts of opportunities. that are going to succeed really well. 2024 is also an election year, and so consumer sentiment is always going to go down on an election year, maybe especially an election year like this one. But the bright side to that is, again, when consumers are world-weary because of climate change and increased natural disasters and the destructive forces of weather becoming worse and worse. And then the general uncertainty of the political situation, I think you're gonna see certain outdoor categories over indexing growth because of people's inclination to prepare for the worst or emergencies and those sort of things. So I look at that stuff broadly and say it's like reason to be optimistic as a business owner, as a leader of a brand, as folks who develop products for that sort of use case. I think longer term though, the things that you have to be worried about are, you know, now that we've kind of democratized the outdoors, we've introduced all these new consumers and casual participants into it. And the best of our outdoor brands have been collaborating with the fashion brands and lifestyle brands. There's no real reason for people to have to come to these core outdoor brands anymore, you know, to be able to have their needs fulfilled. I mean, you'll see things like this quiet outdoors trend which is sort of the spiritual successor to the GORP Core trend, that I think kind of put a lot of outdoor brands on the map in sort of a lifestyle retail setting. Those brands can get the same technical fabrics and the same technical installations, and maybe they don't have the same credibility in that space. Maybe they're not as invested in the endemic use case or the end consumer. But they have the same ability to compete for factory space and the same ability to compete for mindshare in a very busy ecosystem. And so I think that that inevitably is going to force the industry to really have a bit of a moment where they have to refocus on like, what is it that we're going to stand for and what are we going to believe going forward? (16:04.398) There's this fellow, Wes Allen. He used to be the president of Grassroots Outdoor Alliance, and he's just kind of a blue flame thinker who sort of represents the outdoor specialty dealer in that mindset, I think, out in the ecosystem. And he had this really interesting quote from an article that he wrote recently. Authenticity is gathered in drops and lost in buckets, you know, which is really interesting because, like, if you look at the brands that are succeeding right now, I don't know that authenticity is necessarily top of mind. You look at the Stanleys of the world and their incredible growth and their ability to over-index into the popular culture. No idea how long that lasts. Then in the meantime, two years from now, once we've gotten over this hump of our inventory positions, And we're back on that sort of natural progression of where the industry was going to be growing anyways. I'm very curious to see what the industry is going to look like and who's still going to be around and who's going to be relevant and who are the new players, if any, who are going to even be able to break into that space. So you mentioned that because of the lifestyle shift that the industry has taken into the democratization of the product and finding other folks who... who want a North Face or a Patagonia, for example, you see that fashion brands are going to be competing with endemic outdoor brands, and you see that as a threat to part of the future of the industry. I just want to back up to that point, because that was an interesting comment. Yeah, sure. I think that we have actively introduced the Balenciagas and the coaches and the Gucci's into our world. you know, in the spirit of getting earned media and kind of tapping into maybe the intersection of outdoor and street-wear. And that stuff is super cool until all of a sudden you're like, man, there's suddenly a giant influx of technical outdoor apparel and outdoor gear happening in fast fashion, which is the inevitable conclusion, right? You know, it starts with. (18:20.174) the premium brands, but five years from now it's gonna be the Zaras and it's gonna be the Sheeans and all these other folks that are gonna come in and be able to undercut everyone else on price and quality and maybe they don't have the same respect for the IP that's been developed around this sort of stuff. And so I do think about that a lot. It's sort of like, we've sort of been paving our own road, right? And I'll raise my hand on this too. When I was at Chaco, we did some really interesting collaborations with streetwear brands. And they're great and they get you a ton of press and there's like a whole media ecosystem to promote it. But at some point, you'll have a bunch of new folks crowding your category because they see the opportunity. And so ultimately, I think this is where the pressure gets put on not just the brands, but especially the wholesale retail base. I look at the players in specialty and then also bigger players like REI. I think it's going to be a challenging environment to be able to continue servicing the modern consumer and decide where do you want to focus? Do you want people to come to you because you represent the aspiration and the difficult to achieve challenge of being outdoors and how you seek to participate in the outdoors or is it simply a vibe? Cole This is a theory that I have and I'm curious to hear your thoughts on it. I wrote this article and I called it, is your jacket really unique? Josh This is good, yeah. Cole Did you take a look at it? Yeah. Okay, well so my theory is our industry, like we're, we're really good at making products. Like we make exceptional products because we all care about it. We all want, you know, we build product because we want it to perform personally for us on the mountain or wherever we're using it. And so that drives us to innovate. That drives us to push the boundaries of what's possible technically. And you look around at, at all of these companies that have made amazing product, like it's all really good. And I can't tell the difference between technically speaking, like between one brand versus the other brand on a pure technical basis. (20:40.75) And now as you're mentioning, like, From a technical perspective, there could be streetwear brands entering the space as well. Like there's nothing, there's nothing that's left to compete on. Um, if, if the technical details of your product or what you hang your hat on. And for me, then the question is, well, what makes you different? How do you stand out? And it comes in my mind, it comes back to brand, but I'm curious to hear your take on this. Like, is this, is this a real threat with this influx of streetwear that we could foresee into the industry? Josh Yeah, you know, and I would say it's more than just streetwear, right? I think it's just in general, it's fast fashion and the accessibility of trends becoming normalized into, you know, the everyday items that are available to us. I think that, you know, to borrow again from my experience in coffee, coffee was like highly relevant, like really good life lessons learned in my coffee world. But like Specialty coffee is this unique proposition right because like there's Folgers which you can buy in any grocery store But then there's like the specialty coffee shop that will do the pour-over for you and it's like an anaerobic process and You know a natural process that you know The the fruit is literally melting off of the the bean and then it gets roasted and like there's all these things that can can really sell you as a consumer on the idea of like why specialty coffee is better than the basic stuff you can buy in the grocery store. In the same way that there's a difference between natural wine and three buck chuck at Trader Joe's, or the differences between a red and a white wine, or the differences between different types of beer. And I think the challenge is, when you finally double click, you introduce that consumer to that experience and they're like, wow, this is amazing. I had no idea that this was possible. I had no idea that I could get this particular type of technical apparel applied to my product and it's so useful in my everyday life, etc. etc. Or, I didn't know that coffee could taste like this. And then you actually do the doubl -click and it's like, oh, well, this industry is actually pretty small and there's a lot of shared talent and a lot of shared resources, a lot of shared factory base, a lot of shared sourcing. And the reality is, again, the coffee metaphor, it's like we're all buying from the same farms. And we're all buying from the same producers. (23:02.286) And ultimately, the thing that's really differentiated is how you make your product and the label that you put on it. And like label being maybe your brand and like all the values of your company and how you choose to operate your business. You know, but like I would say that, you know, to be super reductive, I think the same thing is kind of true in the outdoor space right now. You know, ultimately, like, you know, if you go on Amazon, you can find a dupe of almost any type of technical garment or any type of outdoor whether it's, you know, like what's the difference between Jackson kayaks or, you know, the sort of thing that you can just find like a rotomolded, you know, kayak on Amazon. I mean, it's very difficult to compete purely on the product or on price. And so ultimately I think that, you know, brand becomes that much more important and not just brand, but maybe like your values and your way of doing business, you know. And Patagonia is an easy example just because, you know, they're the most radical example. You know, for Rumpl, like we have committed to being a 1 % for the planet brand. We've committed to being a B Corp. We've committed to being climate neutral. When we introduced our product, it was white space. It was a new category. Frankly, it was shocking that no one had made it already. But the moment we introduced it, it was a really easy sell-in to consumers and to retailers because it was like, hey, this is incremental dollars. You didn't have this category, now you do. But fast forward a couple years and now we have NPD data that can quantify the size of the prize and any other brands or any other operator can kind of look at it and say, OK, well, maybe we want a piece of that. Doesn't seem very hard. It's not that hard, right? But I think for us, that's what has driven decisions like, OK, we're going to make sure that our product is made with 100 % post-consumer recycled plastic bottles you know, versus just the virgin, you know, materials and factor, you know, the factories have available to them at any given moment. You know, and so like, ultimately, that's the job of, you know, brand and marketing is to kind of like tell that story, make sure we're getting credit for those sort of things. I think what you're going to see in the short term from an outdoor industry standpoint is retailers are really going to cull down the line of products that they actually carry. Everyone from REI down to the independent mom and pops, just a couple doors in like Grassroots Outdoor Alliance. And I think that outdoor specialty will really reassert itself as curators of the best, not the most product. And so I think there will be a few winners in each category. You know, for Rumpl, like for us, it's performance, technical blankets and insulation. This unique product category that we really pioneered and that we've developed and that we're always going to be the market leader because we just had the competitive advantage on it. But I think you're going to see similar things across the board. It's like, do I need to have 20 apparel brands or do I need to focus on eight? You know, like how many brands really need to be competing in a given category? And so I think it's going to be interesting in the short term. I think it will be really hard for new brands to break in. At the same time, you know, for us, it's I have optimism that you're going to see a lot of the bigger players who decided to enter our category probably pull out again, because frankly, it's not really worth their time or energy. You know, in an age of skew rationalization, everybody's going to refocus on their core consumer and the thing that they do really well. Cole So with this wildfire that's blazing. How do you feel like it'll affect the relationship between challenger brands and established brands? Will it thin out the challengers? Will it cripple the established brands to some degree and make room for challenger brands to take their place? How do you see that playing out?", "token_estimate": 3773, "speakers": ["Josh"], "start_timestamp": "11:27.118", "end_timestamp": "11:27.118", "podcast_index": 12, "episode_name": "Ep 12: Josh Weichhand | Finding and Using your Voice | Chac‪o", "slug": "ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's something about having this one really great pair of sandals that have been with me through it all and then getting to share it with people professionally.\"", "podcast_length": "44:34"}, {"id": "6cc08389-8e1e-4ede-831c-8425061d25ed", "episode_id": "3bec55a4-71e0-40b7-a9a0-00c9d02fb78d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482d", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Josh (27:37.934): Yeah, I mean, I do think, I think it's gonna be a little bit of both. Challenger brands, especially in the first few years of their existence, they're very tenuous.I mean the fact that so rumple is celebrating our 10 year anniversary this year and really that's an incredible milestone I mean the fact that we've been that successful that we're around that we've had an incredible period of growth over the last you know, four or five years That's you know, that's something we're celebrating I do think that for a lot of smaller brands, you know And I would say like we've probably you know, we've leveled up at this point You know, we're we're part of that last graduating class of like a lot of new innovative challenger brands, the Topo Designs, the Howler Brothers, you know, BioLite, Peak Design, like a lot of these folks that kind of showed up, you know, for the first time or launched on Kickstarter like we did. But newer brands, I think, you know, cash on hand, your ability to access a supply chain, like these are all really challenging things, you know, to operate even in normal circumstances, let alone when retailers aren't taking chances on new brands or unproven. propositions, right? And so, and then I think, you know, with regards to bigger established companies, I think we're in this interesting age of consolidation right now. Rumpl's kind of unique in that we haven't participated in that. We're still independent. We're still privately held. And there's some really awesome advantages to that. But, you know, like Vista Outdoors, you know, like it's being it's being kind of sectioned off and it'll be sold and it'll be under new ownership. And like, who knows what happens then, right? International ownership. I think that some really big players are gonna need to make the decision of like, are they gonna become a true portfolio company or are they going to be some sort of hybrid? Like Yeti just acquired Mystery Ranch. And that's a really interesting acquisition if you decide to be a portfolio company. If not, it's just kind of like, it's YETI and Mystery Ranch you know, and VF Corp, I think, is going through its own situation right now where, you know, I would imagine that they are starting to look strategically at their portfolio. And they've signaled this, you know, in their earnings calls and everything else. But I think you're going to start to see some significant divestments in a lot of the big anchor global companies and portfolios that really dominated the landscape over the last, call it like five, ten years. I think you're going to see some pretty significant shakeups. And so it's really interesting. I think like three years from now, the dominating players on the landscape might look a little different, you know, or at least like down at the category level. I think you'll start to see a lot of shake-up. Do you feel like this will expedite the shake-up or will it delay things? I think the wheels are in motion for a lot of this stuff right now. I think and it's also the combination of and this is the other thing that I think was kind of driving this whole situation is, you know, the stock market. (30:03.214) Right. Like during the during the pandemic, there wasn't much else to do but play the stock market, you know. And so a lot of brands that were benefiting from that current behavior, their stocks skyrocketed. And now, you know, you're seeing even brands that are really strong on the fundamentals, you know, companies that have a lot of cash on hand that are doing buybacks and everything else. Like they just can't they can't break out of that the current cycle and get back to their previous pandemic high. And I think because of that, like you're starting to see a lot of activist investors get involved. You're starting to see more scrutiny on a lot of the acquisitions and growth plans that were put in place maybe like three years ago or over the last couple of years. You know, like if you're VF and you're trying to justify a two billion dollar acquisition of Supreme, you know, like. I mean, it's going to be an interesting time. So I think to answer your question, I think all the wheels are in motion. I think we're going to start to see the results of that in the next 18 months or sooner. Cole You mentioned something else in one of our intro calls that couldn't be controversial. You said, you asked a question, do brands need to continue to grow? Or can they remain relatively the same size? What's your take there? Do we need to be seeking growth constantly or what is what is slow and steady look like? Josh Yeah. Yeah. I mean, one of the conclusions, I think, from the incredible job that the Outdoor Industry Association has done, like quantifying the size of the opportunity is that now that you've quantified it and you've expressed how large it is, now you feel like you have to make it bigger. And I think the question is just going to be like, OK, how much growth is actually possible? (32:23.182) And I'll go back to my time at Chaco. I think you know Chaco when I was there we had an incredible growth run and you know for for your listeners who aren't familiar like Chaco is a a sports sandal primarily, you know, that was our the core skew a sports sandal that was invented by whitewater rafting guides and it had a lot of utility and it was incredibly comfortable and it was adopted by some of these subcultures, you know that led to some incredible growth over the course of five of the years when I was there, we went from, I think we grew the business by 5X, to the point where there was a moment where Chaco was more than $100 million in US sales. And frankly, I think that was too big for Chaco. I don't think Choco deserved to be that big. And also it was because most of that size was concentrated in the one skew. It wasn't a mix of categories and other products you know, pivoting to find other opportunities to service that consumer that has fallen in love with you. But the problem is, is, you know, looking at one hundred million dollars when you're in a publicly traded company, inevitably it's like, cool, you're one hundred million. You need to be three hundred million. Right. When instead of you're one hundred million. Wow, this is great. This probably won't last long. Like you're probably organically maybe like a sixty million dollar brand, you know, but like this is great while it lasts. You know, how are we going to invest these resources to set you up for the future? You know, so I think that that's a little bit of it, you know, this There's there definitely it feels to me like there needs to be a bit of a mindset change, you know around like, you know Growth is healthy and I do think you know brands are like sharks. Like if they stop moving forward they die And there's no way around that I think in a capitalist system, but I think that having a healthy Outlook on what growth looks like and where it's gonna come from and managing your inventory position and your selling strategy in a way that maybe leaves consumers wanting more versus leaving retailers holding the bag or having to stuff channels with closed-out products. I think that this is something that every single brand in our industry right now is facing and I think you're gonna start to see a lot more discipline in that respect. Well with. I mean, growth for the sake of growth often leads to a lack of focus, a lack of clarity. And I think once you lose focus, things start to kind of fall apart to some degree. And I think like maybe we're seeing that as, as companies are reaching into skews and maybe historically they haven't offered product into.", "token_estimate": 1919, "speakers": ["Josh"], "start_timestamp": "27:37.934", "end_timestamp": "27:37.934", "podcast_index": 12, "episode_name": "Ep 12: Josh Weichhand | Finding and Using your Voice | Chac‪o", "slug": "ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's something about having this one really great pair of sandals that have been with me through it all and then getting to share it with people professionally.\"", "podcast_length": "44:34"}, {"id": "4bd5c1bc-91ae-4b49-a15f-37d9e60bfb9c", "episode_id": "3bec55a4-71e0-40b7-a9a0-00c9d02fb78d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482d", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole (34:47.277): I'm curious, like what is, what is focus? Like what is the value of focus for a brand? What is the value of self-restraint and discipline that's channeled through focus? Like when you have this opportunity, it's hard to say let's pump the brakes. Let's not dive into this next opportunity. What does it take to think that way? Josh discipline and also just maybe a Maybe like a stronger understanding of like your success in the first place, you know, and I think the further out you get from Your early days the more challenging that is, you know rumple is ten years in right? So like this wasn't that long ago when we started, you know on Kickstarter and people were just like this is cool object how this not exists, you know and, you know, I think, you know, just being really transparent, I think we fell into this trap a little bit too where, you know, focus for us was, well, we're going to be the blanket brand. But because we're the blanket brand, we're going to make all sorts of iterations of blankets. And some of these are going to be really difficult to differentiate, you know, whether it's price point or feature set or, you know, the type of insulation that we're using or something else. And there comes a point where it's like, OK, like maybe you don't need to have this many even within your category that you're known for, right? And I mean, that's a very specific example for us. You know, we are in a moment right now where we're doing some really exciting work on our line plan and we're sort of recalibrating and figuring out exactly what is the appropriate mix and at what pricing tiers and at what feature sets to make this whole category and this whole business make sense, not just for the financials, but as a consumer proposition, you know, so it's like very clearly understood. You know, I think my time at Yeti was really formative for me because I think that that was a that is a brand that from the very beginning understood their core consumer and they were building products for that core consumer. You know, starting with, you know, and Yeti, people forget because Yeti is so big now, but like the first 10 years of Yeti's existence was a white, rotomolded cooler. They didn't even have other colors, let alone, you know, drinkware and luggage and go boxes and all the other add-ons. (36:48.366) But I think that the natural progression of their category development, their product development, flowed out of observing and listening and paying attention and servicing that core consumer. So like, I'll use this as an example, but like a Tundra cooler, it's not just a cooler, right? It's the middle seat of a raft going through the Grand Canyon. It's a cutting board you know, for people who are doing saltwater fly fishing, you know, making ceviche on the beach or whatever. You know, it's a stand for people who are, you know, casting, you know, on a skiff. It's a bear-proof safe, you know. And so, like, all of these things, you start to make observations where it's like, OK, how are consumers using this? Well, a lot of our hunting guides and our fishing guides, they store their extra layers and their clothing in the cooler because it's an easy, you know, storage component and they sit on it, you know, and it's like, okay, cool. Well, I feel like we have an opportunity to make chairs, you know, I feel like we have an opportunity to make other, you know, safety closure systems for people to kind of stow their core gear, you know, and I think that that's a really important aspect of maybe what's been missing is allowing the consumer to lead you versus, hey, what just appears to be popular items in the marketplace that maybe we could have a unique point of view on. And I think for Rumpll, for example, we just came out of a really robust consumer study where I think we understand our core consumer, the people who've really lived and breathed Rumpel and who are really excited about our brand and our product over the last several years. And we understand now more than ever what they want to see from us and also how they actually use their product in the day-to-day. And that is now being funneled. through not just the brand strategy, but also the product development process to say, okay, what are the new opportunities that can be created here? And so that, I think, is maybe the way I would probably respond to your question. Discipline is hard. I look at Stanley, and I'm like, Stanley had every incentive to feed that beast and get as big as they have. (39:02.99) And I think, you know, comping those numbers is going to be really challenging for them. But also the reason that they're in that position is because frankly, they had a product that provided a really incredible solution for a core consumer who was really excited about it and talked about it a lot. But that core consumer was not hunters and fishers and construction workers like it was, you know, 50 years ago or even 10 years ago. You know, it was, you know, moms in Utah. who needed something that they could put in a cup holder and that they could drink with one hand and that they could carry with a bag over their shoulder and three kids under an arm. And that I think is more important is like really paying attention to how do people use your products? What problems are you solving for them? And what should naturally flow out of that versus, hey, I just think that we have an opportunity to make a play for this white space over here, this market over here. Cole There's a quote by Bill Gates that I think about often and when asked, what is a skill that you wish you had more of? His answer was, I wish I had more focus. I wish I had the ability to focus. And that's always stuck with me because if one of the arguably one of the most, you know, the smartest people, the wealthiest people in the world, like understands the value of focus and how distracting a lack of focus can be, like, I mean, that seems like it's everything. And I think looking at focus and what that means from like my seat in the industry where like brands will come to us and we'll help them make commercials or docs or something. And oftentimes there's a lack of focus in terms of who the audience is or what the message is or what the call to action is. And like, we always pump the brakes before we actually start making the thing, because we're like, we need to know these answers before we, because like in order to make the thing successful, we have to know what our focus is. And I, I just wonder like focus seems so simple. Obviously, it's way more complicated in actuality. Like, why do we lack focus inherently? Is it because we don't, as an industry, we don't have internal processes to determine focus? Do we just, you know, do we just chase the next shiniest thing? I guess this is a really ambiguous question, but like, why do we lack focus? I can't tell you where I heard this, but it's always stuck with me.", "token_estimate": 1739, "speakers": ["Cole"], "start_timestamp": "34:47.277", "end_timestamp": "34:47.277", "podcast_index": 12, "episode_name": "Ep 12: Josh Weichhand | Finding and Using your Voice | Chac‪o", "slug": "ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's something about having this one really great pair of sandals that have been with me through it all and then getting to share it with people professionally.\"", "podcast_length": "44:34"}, {"id": "db3b38e6-5df7-45b4-852c-2b456300d52b", "episode_id": "3bec55a4-71e0-40b7-a9a0-00c9d02fb78d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482d", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Josh (41:19.63): Someone once told me that the word priorities never existed until recently. It was priority. What is the priority? As in, like, there's one thing that you're focusing on versus, like, we have all these priorities. And I've been having conversations with my direct reports this week, actually, just about we have a lot on our plate because, you know, Rumpl's going through a significant period of transformation. There's really exciting things that we're doing. We're updating our brand platform, you know, our color palette or look and feel, we're rolling out new products, we're preparing for the future, and it requires a lot of work. And so the comments I've made to my team is, listen, nobody's gonna accuse you of not doing enough work or no one's gonna look at the pile of work that you have in front of you and say like, wow, that's not a very big pile. That's not the problem. I think the question is, what are the things in that pile that are actually going to move the needle for the business and better position you for the moment? How can you hold those things out and say, like, this is the thing that is most important right now, or we're facing this particular critical issue as an organization, as an industry, whatever. How can we use the things that are already on our list of to-dos to take bites out of that bigger problem in the process? And so it is always humbling to hear that the wealthiest, most important people in the world or in the economy have the ability to even reflect and say, like, I need to have focus. Like, I marvel at it because I'm by no means important in the grand scheme of things, but I'm like, man, I wish I was in fewer meetings. I wish I had more time to actually like focus and really think about things and really untie the knots of these problems. And it's just a, I think it's just a daily challenge, you know. But ultimately, I think the best advice anybody can have is just, you can only really care about maybe two or three things really at a given time. Yeah, there's all sorts of other things that are gonna crowd your space and crowd your mind, but I think true focus is you're only gonna be able to tackle two or three problems meaningfully. And so how you decide what... (43:39.982) those problems are that are worthy of your attention and worthy of the energy and bandwidth of your organization? I think that's the question that needs to be answered. So earlier you mentioned that there's kind of two challenges the industry faces near term, long term. Near term is kind of the inventory challenge. Long term though is where you see the future of the industry going, the confluence between streetwear and fast fashion and the outdoor industry. I'm curious if focus is part of the secret ingredient to help brands. make it through this wildfire, both in the near-term challenges and the long-term challenges. Is that a reach or you feel like there's some truth to that? I think that's true. And I'm going to respond maybe to one specific situation in a way that might feel like I'm talking out of both sides of my mouth, but I actually don't see any conflict with it. And it's this, that, and I'll use the Rumpl example. Like we... I think we can be laser focused on our core consumer in building the right products for their use cases and their endemic pursuits and the environments that we know that they use them in and respond to the areas that they would like to see us grow. I think we can do that and at the same time there is a rumble solution for consumers who are not necessarily that core consumer. And I think that in the short term that is probably the connecting of dots that needs to happen You know from a from a brand strategy and a sales strategy standpoint You know for us I'll borrow from from Yeti for an example But like you know the the roadie cooler is like a it's like a hard cooler with a handle and wheels really cool product and The way that Yeti brings that sort of thing to market is somebody dragging this thing through the woods for miles over roots and rocks and then they get to the incredible hidden beach and like awesome. You brought your meal, you brought your beverages, you brought the reward for this incredible pursuit and like isn't that cool? But the reality is like 95 % of consumers are gonna use that. They're gonna roll out of their minivan, they're gonna roll it to the sidelines at a soccer game and they're gonna serve orange slices out of it. (45:53.966) And so in that, I think is a little bit of the, you know, who are you making it for versus who wants to buy it? That is a slightly different way to articulate, I think, you know, the strategy for how you address this situation we're in. I think Rumpl has a giant opportunity to, you know, provide outdoor and element proof comforts for people who are enduring, you know, weather and cold and uncomfortable experiences in bleachers and on sidelines and on road trips and on airplanes and all these other things. And those are really natural extensions and adjacencies of the product that we make. But we're making the product for one particular use case. And it just happens to be good for these other things. And I think that's the important thing to keep in mind. There's a lot here. Cole There's a lot of interesting things to dig deeper on and threads to pull. This wildfire is going. Uh, where do you, so, I mean, at the end of it all, like, where do you feel like it puts us? Like, are you expecting to see brands fall out of the wayside companies to disappear and those that can, that can make it through? Like do they come out on top and they come out stronger than ever? You mentioned the very beginning of this conversation that you guys are at Rumpel are trying to figure out like, we can't obviously prevent the wildfire, but how do we build a company that can adapt not only to this one, but future ones? Like Where does this look like? Where does this put us on the other side? Josh Yeah, I mean, for Rumpl in particular, I think you're going to see us be just hyper -disciplined around how much product we're putting out into the market and how we forecast. And I mentioned it earlier, I think that there's something good about leaving consumers wanting more. Like what an incredible problem to have. Like, wow, you bought us out. Cool. Like, stay tuned for the next, right? And so like I would like to, I think everybody would like to see their demand planners and their sales function air more to that side than to the side of like we're gonna bet on giant growth and have no plan for achieving it, right? I think the other portion of what you're talking about is sort of like what are the predictions for the next 18 months? And I think my predictions are probably, (48:14.606) You're going to see a lot of the marquee players in our industry express publicly that they are going to refocus on their core consumer and their core assortment. So you're already starting to see this. There was a there was a news report out from the recent earnings call for Black Diamond where they're basically saying like we're going to shrink and we're going to be a climbing brand. We're going to focus on being an incredible climbing brand. You know, and like they're part of a portfolio with Arcterix and some other brands and everything else. But I think. You know, a portfolio mentality where they're not cannibalizing each other's opportunities, I think is really important. You're going to start to see, I think, some strategic assessments and divestments in portfolio companies, figure out exactly like, OK, what is the right mix to have? You know, Wolverine Worldwide has, you know, Merell, got Saucony, they got Chaco. You know, they have a few other brands, but like In a previous iteration of events, I think you would have seen them all making competing product and trying to sell into similar distribution. I think you're going to start to see more strategy where brands are allowed to cater to the thing that they do best. I think that right, wrong, good or bad, there's going to be less category representation out in wholesale, at least in the short term. People aren't going to take bets on things that are unproven. (50:33.966) Or at least like it's going to have to be a really compelling proposition for them to to make those decisions. And so you'll see the outdoor retailer really refocus their assortment. And then also, you know, sporting goods is going to get out outdoors, I think, like post pandemic. Like you're always starting to see this. Like I think Dick’s is signaling this stick sporting goods. They've got their public lands concept. They made the acquisition of Moose Jaw. They're making some moves there. I think you're going to start to see concepts you know, again, in the spirit of focus, they're gonna focus on the thing that they cater to really well. And then other channels are gonna focus on what they do really well, you know? And so I would anticipate that, you know, 12 months from now, 18 months from now, I actually think Outdoor Specialty will be better off than they've been in a while, just because I think that their role, you know, on the local level and also being able to curate the best collection of gear to service a variety of pursuits versus like, hey, here's four or five different brands all kind of competing on similar terms and similar price or whatever. And having to be educated about those minute differences, I think that's a losing proposition. So I think you're going to see a lot of things clean up on that side as well. Cole Yeah, interesting. Well, as you said, a forest fire is destructive, but it's good for the environment. It cleans up debris. It cleans up some of Not to say that brands are a mess, but it tidies things up and ultimately it allows for new growth and new opportunity. So while it feels like things are hot and heavy and destructive and maybe falling apart, it sounds like there's a lot of reasons to be hopeful. And as you are being optimistic about some of this stuff, it's really just a matter of trying to make it through and come out on the other side a little stronger. I appreciate you sharing and kind of breaking down some of this stuff. Is there anything that we haven't talked about that you want to leave our audience with before we sign off for the day? Josh I think just, you know, one of the reasons I met Rumpl is because of all the brands that sort of graduated into the industry, you know, a decade ago, 15 years ago. I think Rumpl is really interesting and I think we have a core product proposition that's inherently useful and utilitarian. And I'm really excited about the things that we're working on you know, so, you know, in the sense that you've given me a platform to kind of share my point of view on what's going on in the industry, I just want to, you know, give a shout out to this incredible brand that I have the privilege of stewarding and the exciting things that we're doing, you know, on the wholesale front and the investments that we're making, you know, in retail, you know, new brand platform and how we're rolling out kind of a new vision of who we are and what we can be. And that refocus on the core consumer because ultimately, you know, I'm in this for the long haul. I think most of us are. That's why we're in this industry. And we want it to be healthy. And I think we all want to be in a situation where we're not surviving these events, but we're well prepared and adapted to endure and thrive.", "token_estimate": 2882, "speakers": ["Josh"], "start_timestamp": "41:19.63", "end_timestamp": "41:19.63", "podcast_index": 12, "episode_name": "Ep 12: Josh Weichhand | Finding and Using your Voice | Chac‪o", "slug": "ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's something about having this one really great pair of sandals that have been with me through it all and then getting to share it with people professionally.\"", "podcast_length": "44:34"}, {"id": "1ac085e7-97d5-4f5f-ad8e-6ccec1a271d8", "episode_id": "3bec55a4-71e0-40b7-a9a0-00c9d02fb78d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482d", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole (52:47.086): So well said if folks want to follow along with you or with any of the new changes to come on the rumple front Where can people find you? Yeah, so you can find Rumpl at rumpl.com at go rumple on social media give us a follow on Instagram. We're doing some fun things over there. You can find me on LinkedIn Josh Weichhand and difficult to spell and pronounce my last name, but give it a give it a college try and I'm sure you'll find me. Well Josh thank you again. I hope I see you on the podcast sooner than four years from now, but I appreciate you sharing and it's been fun to see what you've been up to. And the amount of progress and change that I think seen in you personally from those Chaco days to now is, it's pretty cool. And speaking of trajectory and the future of where the industry is going, it's neat to see where you're fitting into that as well. So thanks for the time and I hope you have a great rest of your day. Josh Thanks Cole, appreciate it. All right, see ya.", "token_estimate": 251, "speakers": ["Cole"], "start_timestamp": "52:47.086", "end_timestamp": "52:47.086", "podcast_index": 12, "episode_name": "Ep 12: Josh Weichhand | Finding and Using your Voice | Chac‪o", "slug": "ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-12-josh-weichhand-finding-and-using-your-voice-chaco", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's something about having this one really great pair of sandals that have been with me through it all and then getting to share it with people professionally.\"", "podcast_length": "44:34"}, {"id": "857b8459-b187-473f-953e-ed7e3ff9326e", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:00): Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'm going to be your host Today. Brand story is all about the marketing professionals who are working in the industry today. The landscape is constantly changing and evolving, takes really great smart people to run a successful brand, and that's what this podcast is all about. It's all about the people behind the brands we interact with, the challenges they face, as well as their personal journey. So if you're interested in learning more about what it takes to run a top brand, I hope you'll love this podcast and find something educational about it. Enjoy today's episode. Welcome to Brands for my name is Cole Hoborne, and today I have the pleasure of sitting down with Jill Nazi, the director of marketing over at Alta Cycling. Jill, how are you doing?\n\nJill Nazeer (00:40): I'm doing awesome. How are you doing?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:41): Fantastic. You're not too far from where I'm at. You're just down in the Seattle area, is that right?\n\nJill Nazeer (00:47): I am, and actually I'm on the north side of Seattle, so I'm slightly closer to you, but I'm in GreenLake, so a little bit north of all the traffic and everything that Seattle always has,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:57): But currently what traffic, there's not much going on.\n\nJill Nazeer (01:01): That is true. That's the awesome part about everyone being in quarantine is it's a little more green for the planet and also makes our driving a little bit shorter.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:10): Absolutely. So tell us who is Alta. I know you guys manage a bunch of different bike brands that people have probably heard of. They probably haven't heard of Ulta, so fill everyone in.\n\nJill Nazeer (01:22): Yeah, so Ulta Cycling, which is a relatively new company we started existing in August of last year, is a bicycle company based in Kent, Washington, which is about 30 minutes south of Seattle. And like I said, most people would probably not know Ulta cycling, but you would probably know the brands that we own. And those brands are Diamondback Bikes, Raleigh Bicycles, red Line Bikes, high Bike and isip. The latter two are our electric bike brands.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "01:22", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "1720b6e4-72b5-40dc-a6d6-cebdd9b00761", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:54): Wow, so many bikes. Do you own all of them?\n\nJill Nazeer (01:58): No, and if I did, I'm pretty sure that actually my husband would be thrilled, but our neighbors would probably not be happy if we'd have bikes spilling out into the sidewalks.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:07): Fair enough, fair enough. So tell me a little bit about your role. What do you get to do? What do you get to play with as the director of marketing?\n\nJill Nazeer (02:15): I get to play with everything. So my role at Alta is, so I'm the marketing director, so I manage the marketing team and I say that loosely because we are three people, which is surprising for a lot of people when you see that it's five brands. So I manage a marketing manager and she works specifically on high bike and Isep our e-bike brands as well as a field marketing person who actually lives up in Bellingham up near you. And so I personally also manage the other three brands. So I am the brand manager for Diamondback, Raleigh and Redline.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:55): Okay, gotcha. It's a small team, but you guys do a lot of great work. I think what's really cool too is the brands that you guys work with have been around for a really long time. How long has Raleigh been around?\n\nJill Nazeer (03:07): Raleigh's been around since 1887. So for those who don't know the very brief history, it originally started in the uk, but Raleigh USA has obviously been around for a very long time. So it's a lot of fun and very different from the electric bike brands. It's a perfect compromise to have this brand that has over 130 years of history and a history in the Tour de France. And so a lot of people know Raleigh from its heritage.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:34): One of the things that I find really fascinating about your job is just you're a small team, yet you manage five different and very distinct brands. I have a few questions about that idea, but I think the thing that I want to try to talk about is brand differentiation and how you manage all of those. What makes them different and how do you guys organize all of them? I guess let's start there.", "token_estimate": 529, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "01:54", "end_timestamp": "03:34", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "e8d77bc8-360d-4c75-8a7e-44c1d8f5b6b7", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Jill Nazeer (03:58): Yeah, that's a great question. So as far as what makes them different, these brands, again, some of them have had a very long history and they've been different. No brand I think has ever really stayed true to its one to one thing, especially with bikes which are constantly evolving and there's new technology, the brands have to evolve with that. So as far as separating out the brands, we have put a lot of work into making sure they are all different. I mean, in the end, we all sell bikes and although the bikes are different and the product is different and they have a different end use, it's still a bike. And so you have to think, okay, well how can you possibly make that look different five different ways? So especially when I started, which was just a couple of years ago, we really sat down and we put a lot of work into how are all of these brands different? For some it's completely based on product. For instance, high Bike is an electric mountain bike brand. That's what it is. I zip is kind of more of like a California Cruiser e-bike brand. Those brands are not going to be confused from a product standpoint. (05:13): So when my colleague Annie came on, she was tasked with making them look stylistically different and the marketing plans were different. And then for the non-electric bike brands, it's almost a little bit more challenging because these brands have had a lot of overlap. Raleigh has some mountain bikes and Diamondback is known as a mountain bike brand, but also had some road bikes and Diamondback was formally A BMX brand and Redline now has BMX. So you do can't ignore a brand's heritage, but you do want people to know that these are separate distinct brands. So we put a lot of work into it. And where we've shaken out is redline is BMX, people know the history, it's been around since the seventies, so a lot of people know the race history of Red Line. For Diamondback, it's been Road and Mountain bike and Gravel and we focus a lot on, we call it the dirt story, it's gravel and it's mountain, but we put a lot of work into Diamondback placing itself as it's kind of like your friend on the trail. (06:28): We're not the highest end mountain bike brand. We're also offering a variety of price points. We have low price point bikes and mid and high, so we try to make riding a lot more accessible. So we always say we're your buddy at the end of a trail that you high five and you have a go have a beer with. So that's where we've really positioned Diamondback. We want it to be a community feel. We don't just want to sell a bike and never see anybody ever again. And then for Raleigh it's a lot more diverse. We have tons of product in Raleigh and it's a lot more of that history and that heritage of the Raleigh fan. We have people that have been riding Raleigh their whole lives. It's definitely a family brand and stylistically it's fun and light and bright. And so we put a lot of work, both the marketing managers and the design team into how those brands are going to look and feel different when we talk to people.", "token_estimate": 773, "speakers": ["Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "03:58", "end_timestamp": "03:58", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "223e510a-bf10-4c72-b20b-00cde25c6755", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:22): Yeah. You mentioned that a lot of these brands have a ton of backstory and they've been on their own journey for who they are and what they offer. And I know some brands they're looking at wanting to change their product offering or their market, but they have this legacy client base and maybe it doesn't fit where they're trying to go, but how does someone in that position, how do they change their focus? How do they change their brand without losing what they've already built but being able to tap into that new segment they're trying to get into?", "token_estimate": 144, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:22", "end_timestamp": "07:22", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "81af705f-5b48-4460-8da2-688decb3a5be", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Jill Nazeer (07:56): That's a fantastic question and it's an interesting one. And I will say it's interesting not just from the consumer standpoint and how you look externally, but it's an internal thing that we have to deal with first. Our company is very small. People would be shocked to know that there's only about 40 people that currently work at Alta Cycling and that's everything. So 40 people managing five brands. So first of all, think about it that way, that having 40 people that can think five different ways (08:34): Is a truly unique situation. I've worked at brands or at companies where every brand is siloed and every brand has its own team, and those teams do not overlap because they compete with each other. And that's not how it is at Ulta. We all work together. And so the first challenge is kind of what we were talking about earlier where you're selling a bike and how can you make it look five ways of different? So then you have the team of people that are all also trying to think five different ways that adds another level of complication to it. And then we look at the people that are all putting this all together. And someone like myself has been with the company for a couple of years, but my creative director for instance, has been with the company for 15 years. And it's fantastic to have that mix of someone who's a little bit more new and maybe has a fresh perspective and someone who's been with the company for a very long time and can speak to the history and the how's and the why's so we can learn from the successes and the mistakes. (09:44): And then even above and beyond that, we have people who have been in some ways involved with these brands for their entire lives. There were probably red line riders when they were kids or their first bike was a Diamondback or whatever. So you have everyone's personal passion that also adds in this level of complication. So I'll tell you that even internally, we all sit down and when we talk about what is the new product, we are a product driven company. And then we also talk about do we want to do some kind of heritage throwback bike? (10:18): Do we want to take a step back in history and intentionally to speak to those people who know our history? And then there's always an internal debate in that of like, is it right to take a step back in time? Do we only want to move forward? Are we moving forward so fast that maybe our consumer won't keep up with that speed? It's always these different answers. And so it's always a debate, and I honestly think that our product team along with us, they get feedback from everybody does a really great job at which brands can move forward faster. For instance, the Diamondback consumer, they're very knowledgeable about product. They know what the newest spec is, they know what they want. If we don't have a bike that they want, they have no problem telling us. And we listen. I really do tell the product team the feedback we get from the consumer and they're out there talking to people every day, listening to them. So for Diamondback, it makes a little more sense to push technology further and faster. For a brand like Redline, people really want their old school BMX race bike. So it's a great and really complicated question because you don't want to alienate the people that have loved you their whole lives, but you also do of course have to move forward. So a lot of it is based on making sure you're offering a little bit of something for everyone without making it look like you are just catering to them. You want to make it look intentional. (11:59): When Raleigh had a whole heritage line a few years ago, and that was a conscious decision to say, Raleigh, this year is going to make this product and we're going to speak to our heritage and celebrate it. And so I think that as long as you're putting that message out, people get excited about it and I think the consumer enjoys being along that ride with us.", "token_estimate": 992, "speakers": ["Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "07:56", "end_timestamp": "07:56", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "d319b2be-19e6-496a-a21c-79e8c2424876", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:19): I see you did it. That was a good pun. You like that?\n\nJill Nazeer (12:22): I got a lot of good bike puns. We'll see how long this keeps going for.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:27): No, that's a really great answer. Yeah, I can only imagine how challenging, but also the opportunity that arises when you have such a small team and you have to figure out how to navigate these complicated questions. So no, that's cool. How do you know when you have too many brands, if you're a parent brand and you're looking at, let's say you got, I don't know, 20 or 10 or however many, when do you merge and when do you separate them? What are some indicators?\n\nJill Nazeer (12:55): Yeah, that's a good question. I think there's a business answer to that and a brand answer to that.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:01): So", "token_estimate": 203, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "12:19", "end_timestamp": "13:01", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "dc092c62-8c21-49c1-82bb-bb6d8f60da6a", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Jill Nazeer (13:01): I'd say the business answer to it is there's definitely a fine line between a halo effect where one brand will help lend some light to the others and cannibalization of when one brand's sucking up the sales of another. And I think it's completely okay to walk that line, but I think that when you see that a brand is starting to cannibalize way too much, that's when you have to make some hard decisions. And certainly that's happened and that's why brands change and that's why some brands disappear. And that's why sometimes it's the product that changes. And from a business standpoint, sometimes companies make that decision in different ways. For us, right now, we make that decision based on product. We have different products for different brands and that's how we keep them separated. But in the past, there was a time when they alter their distribution based on the brand. So one brand might only be in a retail shop and one brand might only be sold online, and they thought that was the solution to too many brands. And I think that the retail landscape has changed so quickly in the last, even just the last five years, that it's difficult for companies to look at the different brands they offer and try to make a channel strategy based on it because frankly now everybody sells almost everywhere. So that's how we are. We consider ourselves an omnichannel company. So from the business side of things, we want to support the local retailers, we want to support online sellers, and we sell direct (14:49): And with all five brands. So then the next question is, well, the next question was how do you separate the brands and when is it too much? Well, that's why now we have differentiation in product. So (15:03): That's why for right now, Diamondback doesn't sell BMX redline does. I'm not going to say that there won't ever be overlap in product, but certainly sometimes you can separate it as much as you want. And then from the marketing standpoint, and this is just from my seat, is that sometimes I know it's a little too much. When you start to feel yourself falling down that hole of almost being robotic, we laugh about it a little bit. So I manage three different brands that have three very different voices, and I think a lot of people are surprised when they learn that it's me that's the one behind all of those voices. It makes me sound a little bit psychotic sometimes, but I find at times, for instance, right before a Black Friday sale, when I have 50 pieces of marketing copy that I have to write, and you start feeling this roboticness of sounding like a used car salesman, like sale or best sale ever, biggest sale of the year, come get your bike When you know that you yourself are starting to feel robotic and when it's not really you and how you would speak as that brand, that that's when it's a problem. (16:24): And so that is why it's so great sometimes to either take a step back and examine what you're really doing and what the goals of that brand are, or you look and you say, okay, maybe this is too much for this brand, and then you reevaluate what your plans are.", "token_estimate": 784, "speakers": ["Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "13:01", "end_timestamp": "13:01", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "4c2925e5-657f-47cb-8950-c8833ed82ae9", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:39): Interesting. So for the brands that have figured out how to differentiate, how do you guys then adjust your media plans for each brand to reflect those brand values? What would you say to someone who's trying to, they figured out the first part, now they're trying to figure out how does media fit into this?\n\nJill Nazeer (16:59): That's a really good question. So depends on if it's traditional media or paid media, which we do both. Traditional media is a little easier. There are electric bike specific publications that the e-bikes would be in or something like a bicycling magazine that does a really great job of covering every kind of bike. So that's something that Raleigh might be interested in or Isaac. Then for a brand like Diamondback, which is a little bit more tech and a little bit more dirt focused, that's when you go to the media that is also a little bit more tech and dirt focused things that help grow your community that are already there. And then from the digital media perspective, it is interesting. There's again been different iterations and different theories on should every brand get to be in digital media because then aren't we cannibalizing our own buy and aren't we competing with ourselves? And\n\nSpeaker 3 (18:04): This", "token_estimate": 320, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jill Nazeer", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "16:39", "end_timestamp": "18:04", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "7f549361-1eef-499a-94f4-d7cd75b90ef7", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Jill Nazeer (18:04): Is why some companies keep their different brands on separate teams so they don't risk cannibalization. It's almost like competition within your own company to see who can do better. For us. I'm kind of glad we don't do that. I would rather all of us work together so we all know what the other one is doing. So I'd rather just all play nice in the sandbox together. So we luckily have a really great buying team that helps us with this, but it is making sure that you're walking that delicate balance between addressing your existing community. Again, for Diamondback, it would be like somebody who might already be interested in mountain biking, but especially for bikes, you also have to grow the community. Buying a bike is something that most people would only do maybe a couple of times in their life, and it's an expensive purchase. It can be a very emotional purchase for people, and you kind of have to grow the consumer base, otherwise there's too many bike brands you would flat out just run out of people. So the media part of it is challenging because you don't want to ignore your existing community, but you absolutely have to grow the community as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:21): Yeah, interesting. Okay, cool. So let's change gears a little bit and let's talk more about your background. How did you end up here?\n\nJill Nazeer (19:30): So I've had kind of a roundabout way of getting here. So right after, so in college I was an art major and I was an absolutely terrible studio artist. It is frankly shocking that I graduated with a degree in art. Any designer that I've ever worked with or any creative I've ever worked with laughs when I tell them I was an art major. So it's an embarrassment to their profession. So I started out doing that and then I got a master's degree in humanities actually focusing in art history, which obviously my job has nothing to do with right now, but that's okay.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:11): I mean, you guys work with 120 year old brand that has history, right? That's true.", "token_estimate": 511, "speakers": ["Jill Nazeer", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:04", "end_timestamp": "20:11", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "390afd32-a745-4785-81a3-4c3826a342d7", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Jill Nazeer (20:16): Yeah, that's a little bit of art history right there in itself. I laugh because my art program was a very conceptual art program, so I laugh that being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little bit more creatively even though the art itself wasn't that great. So from there, I am from Chicago originally, and so if you're an art major and you live in Chicago, basically you work at an ad agency, that's what everyone thinks that you should do. This was the age when Mad Men was very big, so that was a natural next step. And I worked at a massive ad agency in Chicago on the MillerCoors account. And so that's my first step into managing a lot of brands at once. MillerCoors owns way more brands than people even realize, and so it takes a lot of multitasking to work on all of those. (21:13): And from working at this massive ad agency, I decided that I need a karmic balance in my life. And I totally switched gears and went to go work at a nonprofit and I worked at the Leukemia and Lymphoma Society. They have a campaign, it's a revenue program for them called Team in Training. At the time, it was the world's largest endurance training fundraising program. So essentially we had coaches and training programs and we would train you for some massive endurance event like the Chicago Marathon or the Chicago Triathlon, and in exchange you would fundraise for the organization. (21:57): So although I was a fundraiser, it really was my experience with managing events and customer service because you're just managing a lot of people and grassroots marketing, because it's a nonprofit and every dollar you make needs to go to the mission. So you really have to get creative and make sure that you don't mind getting a little bit grassroots trying to get marketing done. So that was truly a lesson in how to do a lot with nothing. From there, I took a role as an account marketing rep for the North Face's Sales Agency. So at the time, the North Face didn't have any internal salespeople, it was all external agencies. So I supported them essentially as a field marketer. So any of the non-North Face stores, like small independent retailers and bigger stores like Dander Mountain and Shields and all those, I would work on the marketing for them. So I was employed by an agency, but worked a lot with the North Face's marketing team, and that was awesome. The North Face is just such a cool, fun brand, and they're so far ahead of technology and their teams are so creative. It was a lot of fun to get insight into a brand and how it works. (23:18): Even though I was in the field, the North Face makes you feel very a part of their entire family. And so that was my first foray into that. And then in 2016, we moved to Seattle. So from there I worked at K two Sports for a little bit, and I managed two of their Snowshoe brands, Atlas and Tubs, as well as their Nordic brand, which is Modus. So that was three brands all by myself. I was basically a one person marketing team. So that in itself was also a lesson. And then from there went to my role now, which originally was at a company called Excel North America, but now Ulta cycling.", "token_estimate": 799, "speakers": ["Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "20:16", "end_timestamp": "20:16", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "5e743a6b-4262-4b6f-bf6c-f1dd9ace1e22", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:59): Gotcha. If there's one thing that you've picked up in your career career that you take with you or you apply to the next day, is there anything that comes to mind?\n\nJill Nazeer (24:11): Yeah, I mean, I would say this is if I was going to give my niece and nephew advice in life, it would be, and I say this jokingly, that it's totally okay and sometimes actually better to not have a clear path. Obviously, I've kind of done a little bit of everything that all led me to my role now, but I truly believe that not knowing exactly what I was going to do or what was going on has actually made me a better marketer. (24:43): I think that having the ability to have been in all these different roles in different kinds of companies and managing multiple brands simultaneously really teaches you how to take a step further back and see a bigger picture. I think that if I had only been a brand manager on a single brand my entire life, I would've only seen that. I would've only seen that little piece. And I think that it's actually a benefit to work in different places and do all these different things because it really teaches you how to use an outsider's perspective to solve problems. And my dad would probably laugh at that because our parents were all a generation where they stayed in a job for 45 years, and if you saw six jobs on someone's resume, you thought,\n\nCole Heilborn (25:32): What's wrong with them?\n\nJill Nazeer (25:33): Exactly. Like, oh my God, they can't make a commitment to save their life. So I'm so grateful that now that's no longer the mentality that now it's actually a benefit to have people that come from totally different backgrounds to solve these\n\nCole Heilborn (25:48): Problems. Yeah, yeah. What's your hope for the future of the bike industry?", "token_estimate": 451, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "23:59", "end_timestamp": "25:48", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "3c5168c1-6fa9-4b7d-99bc-3a58c342e110", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Jill Nazeer (25:55): My hope for the future, well, I mean, end goal is we want more people riding bikes and to continue riding bikes. If it's an e-bike, it's a non-electric bike. Just getting more people out riding. I mean, I ride bikes all the time myself. I truly do believe that this is something that I hope continues on for future generations so they can keep doing it safely. (26:21): And from an industry standpoint, I think actually Casey might've mentioned this, and I totally agreed, and when you interviewed him on your podcast, that I do hope that the industry as a whole can kind of take a step back and look at what they've done in the past, and that's great, and use that as a reference, but to keep pushing forward into new things and not always rely on the old way of thinking. I think if anything has taught us the last five to 10 years of how retail has been running, is that you can't just hope that things are going to revert back to the way they were because they're not. And just making sure that you try to encourage diversity in the industry, and that's with people, and that's with problem solving as well. Just how do we make sure that this industry can continue to grow and support its future generations, and that might be a new and different solution that no one's ever thought of before.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:28): Yeah. No, that's really well said. And the more people on bikes, the better.\n\nJill Nazeer (27:32): Yeah. It can only help us.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:34): Exactly. I don't see any negative downside.\n\nJill Nazeer (27:37): No bikes are going to solve all the world's problems.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:39): Trust me. I think so. Well, thank you for sitting down and chatting. We're about out of time. It's been really awesome to chat with you specifically just because I remember my first road bike was a Diamondback. I bought it off Craigslist in high school was, I don't remember where it was from or how old it was, but it had the old drop tube shifters.\n\nJill Nazeer (27:58): Oh, sweet.", "token_estimate": 505, "speakers": ["Jill Nazeer", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:55", "end_timestamp": "27:58", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "ee3002ca-6b88-407a-af87-a8aa0cf11910", "episode_id": "0c1bcd9c-3a90-4dc7-b1be-3177484183f9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4832", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (27:58): Yeah, and I put yellow bar tape on it, and man, it was so cool. So personally, this has been really fun to chat and get to know you. If people want to find you, if they want to find, well, any of the five brands, where can they follow along?\n\nJill Nazeer (28:12): So luckily, all of our brands are connected together on our website. So you can go to diamondback.com, rally usa.com, redline bicycle.com, hi bike usa.com, and I zip e-bikes dot com. All those brands are connected, so if you only remember one of them, it's okay. And yeah, and we have separate social media accounts, so feel free to follow us. And I am the one that is posting on social media, so if you reply to something on social media, that is me replying back. I'm a real live human.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:48): You're not a bot.\n\nJill Nazeer (28:48): You talk to me there. I am not a bot.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:51): Perfect. Well, thanks so much, Jill. It's been a great time having you on the show.\n\nJill Nazeer (28:54): Yeah, thank you so much for having me.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:56): Take care.\n\nJill Nazeer (28:57): Thanks.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:58): Thank you for listening to this episode of Brand Story. If you enjoyed today's episode, please consider leaving us a review on Apple Podcast or sharing this with a friend or colleague. Tune in next time for another episode.", "token_estimate": 340, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jill Nazeer"], "start_timestamp": "27:58", "end_timestamp": "28:58", "podcast_index": 14, "episode_name": "Ep 14: Jill Nazeer | Brand Differentiation | Alta Cyclin‪g", "slug": "ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-14-jill-nazeer-brand-differentiation-alta-cycling", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being a bad artist made me a better marketer because it made me think a little more creatively.\"", "podcast_length": "44:36"}, {"id": "db6f7a62-f005-422f-9c66-afb06ac8d291", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:00): Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'm going to be your host Today Brand story is all about the marketing professionals who are working in the industry today. The landscape is constantly changing and evolving, takes really great smart people to run a successful brand, and that's what this podcast is all about. It's all about the people behind the brands we interact with, the challenges they face, as well as their personal journey. So if you're interested in learning more about what it takes to run a top brand, I hope you'll love this podcast and find something educational about it. Enjoy today's episode. Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Holborn, and today I'm sitting down with Brenna Rosenthal, the owner of Great Escape Media. Brenda, how are you doing?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (00:39): Good, how are you?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:41): Fantastic. It's awesome to have you on the show.\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (00:45): Yeah, I'm excited to be here.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:47): Where are you located?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (00:49): I am currently located in Denver, Colorado, in the guest bedroom of my house.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:56): Well, I have to give a quick shout out to Casey over at Raven for introducing us. He is a great guy, and it sounds like you guys have known each other for a long time. I've only just had the pleasure of getting to know him over the last month or so, but he's how we got connected, right?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (01:12): Yeah, Casey Casey's awesome. I've known him for a while just through kind of I guess multiple outdoor industry jobs and connections and always see him at the trade shows and around Denver. So yeah, I'm definitely grateful that he introduced us too. And thanks Casey.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:31): Yeah, thank you Casey. And for the folks listening, if you're new to the show, you might scroll back and find that episode, I think it's number seven or eight. Casey's a great guy with a lot of great personality and a great personal story, but anyways, Brenna, let's chat about who are you guys? What does Great Escape Media? What do you guys do?", "token_estimate": 530, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Brennah Rosenthal"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "01:31", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "08219a51-7868-432d-a7ea-bbe71a8245c3", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (01:49): Sure. So Great Escape Media is a company that I started about two and a half years ago that focuses on creative content, strategy and production, as well as merging the two of those together. So for the confusion that just caused for everyone, creative content strategy is basically taking a brand or company and looking at how they can present content to their audience through things like social media, YouTube, and their website and email campaigns in a creative way. So through imagery, video and creative storytelling. And then on the production side of things, we focus on basically taking that strategy and stuff and bringing it to life through both photo and video production.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:35): Yeah, absolutely. What was the biggest problem that you noticed and I guess that you're trying to solve by jumping out on your own and starting your own company?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (02:43): Yeah, I think one of the big things is that there's these huge agencies that exist and then these brands that have in-house marketing and content people that might not have the resources that some of these bigger brands have. So I think one of the problems I was trying to fix was kind of better helping these brands and companies tell their stories without the muddied Bs, I guess, of a huge agency where they're a million people in a room, a million a opinions, and then also added dollar amount that comes along with using an advertising agency. My whole, I guess, shtick with Great Escape Media is to act more of a friend and a connection versus this big scary agency that a lot of, they do really, really great work, but a lot of the brands today just don't need that robust of a system and are looking for someone they can feel a little bit more personally connected to work on these projects that they're going to get way better and more concentrated work out of versus working with a giant company. So\n\nCole Heilborn (03:53): For sure,\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (03:54): I like to tell all my clients that they can pick up the phone and call me, whether it's having a bad day or they need help with a problem.", "token_estimate": 534, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:49", "end_timestamp": "03:54", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "5115ecdb-c71f-481e-a9aa-57fc96912191", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (04:01): What are the ideal businesses that need services, what you guys have to offer if it's not like the big agencies cover the big corporate clients, where do you find your niche?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (04:12): Yeah, I mean I love working with smaller startup brands that are trying to get their company off the ground, whether that be with a photo or video shoot or with content strategy. But other times too, I love working with bigger brands on productions to make it feel like this production isn't this big corporate structure, that they have more of a personal connection with the producer that they're working with to have onset, that they can feel like they have someone to lean on.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:41): And how long have you been doing Great Escape Media?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (04:44): About two and a half years. So I've always freelanced in addition to the full-time jobs that I had before, but great escape's been a full-time thing for about two and a half years.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:52): Okay, gotcha. The advertising marketing world is definitely an interesting place, and I'm curious if you can kind of speak to just the things that you've observed over your time in the industry because there seems to be the rise of the small production shops or the small strategy shops. You're one of them, our company's one of them. Where and when did all of these small companies come forth and what's the opportunity?", "token_estimate": 360, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Brennah Rosenthal"], "start_timestamp": "04:01", "end_timestamp": "04:52", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "13af01e2-6a55-4612-931f-8272870b7627", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (05:16): Yeah, I mean I think it's an interesting point and one of the main reasons I think is because of that giant dollar amount that comes along with some of these huge advertising agencies. It's like it's paying for a premium, which you do get premium services a lot of the time, but I think nowadays with the cost of entry for advertising itself with Facebook and social media is so much lower than it used to be where you had to pay hundreds of thousands if not millions of dollars to get a TV ad spot. Whereas now you can put a hundred dollars behind a social media ad with a video that was created through a smaller production company and get just as many eyes on it if not more, and get a way more targeted audience for it versus broadcasting a huge commercial to everyone that's watching the news that night and maybe that person's not into Nike and they don't do fitness and they sit on their couch and eat snacks all day. (06:13): So I think that there's been, because of that change with the way that advertising is running now, these companies I guess don't feel like they need to pay that giant premium with these agencies and they would rather work with someone that is going to give their company this full undivided attention for without that premium cost associated with it. It's the same thing that's happening with direct to consumer brands. I would say it's a very similar kind of crossover where you are seeing these brands that are launching in a direct to consumer way because of the fact that you don't need these bigger retail stores to showcase a product. It's very similar in the way that you don't need to pay this extra money for a really good product or service. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (07:00): No, interesting. So with you, your tagline being, but you're not really in-house, the experience been for your clients. Do they like that setup? How does that provide value to them further than just being a contractor?", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:16", "end_timestamp": "07:00", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "0b168937-a0ae-4a32-91bc-18657da0944a", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (07:19): Yeah, I think it's just the fact that they have confidence in knowing that I know their brand really well and am invested in the story that they're trying to tell and that kind of thing, so that it's not just someone that's working on, yes, I do work on multiple brands during the course of the day, but that acting as if I'm in-house makes it feel like I know their brand kind of in and out, and I want to know the brand in and out, asking a lot of questions and making sure that I know every single facet of these brands versus having eight different people working on it and someone knows this about the brand, this about the brand, I want to know everything.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:02): I got to imagine for your clients there's a lot of value and knowing that they have someone they can trust who knows their brand, but also has that outside perspective that you can bring to the table if they're trying to solve something that they're not sure how to approach.\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (08:16): And I think that having multiple brands that I work on helps with kind of diversifying what I have for those certain clients too. So having experience with one client will help then better even another one of my clients. So going through, for instance, running an ad that doesn't do as well for a certain brand, then I'll have that knowledge to bring to another one of my clients that says, Hey, one of my clients is struggling with this business and this, I think we should adjust this. Just having those experience that kind of cross over that I think are really beneficial to having multiple clients. But there is that, I'm like, yeah, it's hard to have this many different clients that I'm working on from different industries, but at the same time, it is beneficial to be able to have that crossover and learn from each different one to then better all of the clients as a whole.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:13): Yeah. Do you think that as you look forward, do you think we'll see more and more of the small shop style popping up? And if so, what does that do to the landscape for options or the ad agency or the ad industry? Excuse me.", "token_estimate": 538, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:19", "end_timestamp": "09:13", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "d4304927-a843-41fd-be96-0db48b8fbeba", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (09:28): Yeah, I think it's interesting for sure. I think I'm so immersed in these smaller shops that I do believe that the smaller shops do better work and that they are going to come to the forefront and are unfortunately putting some of these bigger agencies kind of out of work or having a harder time finding clients because these companies are getting this undivided attention from these smaller shops and for a price that's not as premium. So for background, I am part of a six person creative office space. So basically there's six creative freelancers that split the rent in one office space, and all of us are freelancers, and I am immersed in that kind of small one to two person shop environment on a daily basis because of that. And the cool thing about that too is that because we are all in the same office space, we will work on projects together or recommend each other for certain things. And I think that that is how things should be and that they're going to be moving forward just because like I said, there's this personal touch that you get with these smaller shops that you might not get from these bigger agencies.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:44): For sure. So we've talked a lot about why the small shops are doing a great job and what they can bring to the table. Where do you think the line is drawn in the sand between when the small shop should let the big agency take over and what they can bring to the table? Where is that? Is there a line I suppose?", "token_estimate": 373, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:28", "end_timestamp": "10:44", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "2e35e9e5-8368-494c-95d8-3354e7870a77", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (11:03): Yeah, I mean I definitely think there is. If you look at Super Bowl commercials, yes, I think there are smaller shops that can handle that, but agencies have the resources and people and network to be able to pull something like that off versus if I were to try and do a Super Bowl commercial with Great Escape media, it would be terrifying. I think I could find the resources to do it, but that'd be something where I would call someone that I know that works at a bigger agency that says, Hey, can we partner with so-and-so agency to bring this production to life? So I think it's almost the emerging, I don't know that there's day and night, they're just working completely separately, but the merging of these agencies with maybe smaller production shops. So a huge agency is responsible for the creative and media buy and placement of things, but hire these smaller production shops that are truly very good at what they do to bring those productions to life. So I think the partnership between the two is a very strong force.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:02): So for people that are listening, if they're where you were a couple years ago and they're trying to decide, Hey, I've got this talent, I've got this passion for whether it's strategy or production or social media, and they're considering going down the route that you've gone down and creating your own business, how was it? What's the journey been for you?", "token_estimate": 361, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:03", "end_timestamp": "12:02", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "83c8a4bf-2e0f-4c77-849e-3981a9f101ad", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (12:21): If someone were to come to me to say that they wanted to start their own business? I would definitely say to try it. So if just them shearly having that thought in their head means that they have some sort of resilience or strength to do that kind of thing, versus just someone that's like, I'm comfortable in this corporate job, I would never suggest someone that has that thought process to start their own business. So I think basically what happened is I was in a job for three and a half years that I loved, but always felt the need to live out this dream that I had of starting my own business or being off on my own. So what I did from there is I talked to probably 10 to 15 different people that were freelancers or self-employed or had started their own companies to be like, what do you think I should do? (13:11): I needed to hear that from 10 to 15 people to be like, oh, you can do this. And that's something that I hope I provide to people that ask me the same question that the confidence instilled to say, Hey, you can do this, and if it doesn't work for some reason, you'll have fallback. You could always apply to another job. It might take a little bit to get, but you're not going to just be buried in the ground with no work. So that helped me a lot. Like the people that surrounded me that told me that I could do it, and then also shared their stories with me about how they quit their job, they filed their business name, started their own business. That gave me the confidence to say, Hey, I can do it myself too. I also had the support of my family as well. (13:55): My mom and dad are both business owners too, had the support of them to say, Hey, this is how you have to do these things. You don't know, they don't teach you that in school. They don't teach you how to start your own business in school when you have an advertising photography degree. So I think that the support system around me was huge in that. And then there was one instance at work that just clicked for me one day and I was like, I'm going to quit and do this. I registered a business name, got all my tax paperwork done and created a very mediocre logo, which is still in play, but I'm having it redone right now to get my business off the ground. And from there, what I did is I sent my entire network and email that said, Hey, I am doing this. This is what I'm focusing my time and energy on. I'm super passionate about it. These are the services I can offer. If you or anyone would be interested hit up. And since I sent that email about two and a half years ago, I haven't done a lick of marketing for great Escape media. All of my business has come to me through word of mouth or recommendation.", "token_estimate": 683, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal"], "start_timestamp": "12:21", "end_timestamp": "12:21", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "68c2035a-79e0-4750-8c62-7324086c528d", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:04): Wow, that's awesome. Well done.\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (15:06): Yeah, it it's been exciting. And I think that goes to show that network is so hugely important in starting a business of any kind.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:18): So for the folks that are, they want to start their own business, but let's say they don't have, maybe they're fresh out of college, maybe they don't have that network because they haven't been in the industry for some time, but they're really set on jumping out on their own. How would you suggest people start to build their network?", "token_estimate": 144, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Brennah Rosenthal"], "start_timestamp": "15:04", "end_timestamp": "15:18", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "170f16fc-4293-4a64-be58-a8f61ad96193", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (15:33): Yeah, I mean, I think there's a couple of different ways to build a network. There's obviously platforms like LinkedIn that are actually super helpful if you're able to build that out and start adding people and are not afraid to connect and have conversations with people that are strangers. Instagram's another one of those too, that if you are projecting out that this is what you're doing, a lot of the people aren't afraid to get a direct message on Instagram as like a, Hey, can you get a cup of coffee with me? So I always tell people like that, if you look up to someone or want to connect with them, just ask to get a cup of coffee with them. A lot of people are going to say yes to that. And I make it a point to say yes to everyone who asks me to do that because I did it to so many people leading up to where I am today, that those conversations, even if sometimes they might feel like they take a chunk out of your day that's not worth it, they are always worth it for both parties. (16:25): So there's something to be learned from those people that want the advice of starting off on their own, and also for them to learn from the people that are off on their own. So I think those are two of the hugest things is just reaching out to people to connect. And then sometimes it's a means of taking jobs or projects that are a little bit smaller than you might want to start off with so that you can meet those people and create those network and show how hardworking you are. Networking events are always really awkward, but typically something will come out of it. I always tell people it's like, yeah, my strategy for those is I just post up at an empty table and wait for people to come to me, which might not be the best thing in the world, but it always works.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:10): It depends how friendly you look, I think.", "token_estimate": 468, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:33", "end_timestamp": "17:10", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "cef96342-2f71-4b4b-ba32-a0f6123cac72", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (17:12): Yeah, sometimes I don't think I do. I like to joke that I have rusting bitch face, but I try to cognizant of the fact that I'm sitting there and waiting for people. But there's always that one other person that's there that is in the same boat where they dunno anyone else, they didn't bring a friend with that said networking event, and they'll be like, Hey, can I come to your table? Or I'll be that person if I see someone alone, I'm like, Hey, can I put my drink down here? And then you're like, Hey, what do you do? And\n\nCole Heilborn (17:40): Then you're like, okay, this isn't so bad. I've got a buddy now.\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (17:42): Yeah, exactly. And then it's just building up that network and making sure you work really, really, really from those beginning days, and obviously always to make sure that you show those people that you're working with, that you are capable of doing what you do, so that then they recommend you to someone they know and it creates a snowball effect.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:03): I'm sure when you were going into it, you had a lot of doubt or you had a lot of ideas of the challenges that you would face. Were there any challenges that came along that surprised you?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (18:13): Yes. I think it was having to say no.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:18): Oh, okay.", "token_estimate": 333, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:12", "end_timestamp": "18:18", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "397c71f1-e785-4441-9029-6589040d418b", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (18:18): Yeah. And I still struggle with that of someone that's like, Hey, I want you to work on so-and-so project for $2. And I'm like, oh yeah, what does my income look like this month? Should I work on this project for, it's not $2, but a very well amount in the scheme of things. I think at the beginning I took everything that came to me. I was so afraid that I wasn't going to make money (18:44): That I was like, yes, I'll take this project. And then ended up having clients that just rung me dry. I think a lot of people will say this, that the lower paying ones tend to have more issues and they take advantage of the time more if they're doing that. So I think I said yes to some and got myself into working with some of these clients that were hardly paying me anything and expecting the world of me, and I needed to build up resilience through those clients to be able to say no and set boundaries. Boundaries. Setting boundaries was a huge process of that. And I still struggle and work on that on a daily basis is setting boundaries with clients and making sure I don't say yes to everything.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:29): Yeah. Do you have any, I dunno, criteria or a little checklist you go through before you decide to say yes to something?", "token_estimate": 318, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:18", "end_timestamp": "19:29", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "3fc15e27-7273-49a3-9d1e-c265d34bd481", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (19:37): So this is something that actually has been kind of built with the guys in my shared office space. And even if we're talking amongst one another about taking a certain job, it's like it needs to check at least two or three boxes as is it pay well? Is one of them that dollar sign attractive? The other one is, are you going to meet someone there that is going to help with upgrading your network or future work and is it a good portfolio builder? So if it doesn't check two of the three of those boxes, probably say no to it. And that's kind of how we go through things in the office too. If we're getting proposals from clients that are like, oh, the money's really bad, but this is a super cool project, it'd be great for my portfolio, and this is something that could turn into a bigger project because of that, take it if it's maybe something that's not that exciting to work about, but that dollar sign's really good, take it. If it doesn't check any of those boxes, don't take it.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:39): That's interesting. I think that's actually the same set of criteria that we use to decide if we're going to take a project or not. And I will say, I think there's been so many great creative opportunities that come up, but you are looking at how much money you're making that month and you're like, I really don't know if I should do this. I need to focus on making money. But I found, and I don't know if you found the same thing, but when you take those creative opportunities, they really pay dividends in the future just with the amount of value that they bring to your portfolio or the people that you meet along the way.\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (21:12): Exactly. And I tell the story of, I worked on a music video in the middle of nowhere, and it was probably the worst week of my life just because of everything that happened. It was absolutely insane. I slept maybe a total of four hours in the course of a\n\nCole Heilborn (21:31): Week. Oh my gosh.", "token_estimate": 498, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:37", "end_timestamp": "21:31", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "b6057851-e54c-43bd-a064-ea13919e7b8d", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (21:31): And I came home, I was like, I'm never doing production ever again. And then the video came out and it had 50 million plus views on it, and I was like, okay, that hell week and a half was maybe worth it. But it also showed me how to create boundaries with those kinds of projects too. So it's something that I will talk about. It's funny now looking back on it, during the time I was like, this is miserable and I hate everything about this, but looking back on it that that's a portfolio piece for me that gets me work. So there's some project I'm like, I can only do one of those a year now though.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:10): Yeah, fair enough. Fair enough. Well, I think I was looking at your Instagram earlier, and it looks like you've got some panel discussions that you're a part of coming up. Can you tell us about that?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (22:19): Yeah, so there's been a lot that's come up in the last week of talks including this, which is exciting. I enjoy doing this kind of stuff and helping people out. So next week there's a group that's started in Denver through all of the COVID nonsense called Fireside at five. And what they're basically doing is on a weekly basis hosting these chats that are actually small amount of people and there is a person that leads the discussion. So there's three separate ones that I'm doing with them over the course of the next three weeks. One about Instagram stories and how they're beneficial. Another that I'm partnering with one of the directors I work with, his name's Chris Claire about documentary storytelling in a commercial environment. So that one should be really fun. Chris is incredibly talented and I produce with him a lot, so we're excited for that. And then another one about how to let consumers into your brand in kind of a behind the scenes way, so showing people around the office, that kind of stuff and why that's important for brands these days, especially with social media. And then the other one is, I believe it's next week too, with General Assembly, which is a learning platform for both marketing and the tech industry is really about marketing in 2020 for outdoor industry specific.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:31", "end_timestamp": "22:19", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "72367b6c-09af-4ec5-9e35-f27dc187939a", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:44): Oh, very cool. Very cool.\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (23:46): Moderating. And there's some panelists on that one, and it's free to attend.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:52): That's great. I'm not sure that this podcast episode will be up before your panel. Are these going to be hosted online somewhere? So if this comes out afterwards, people can go and find them after their life?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (24:07): The General Assembly one? Yes. I'll have to check and see about the other ones. So the Fireside at Five ones are, like I said, very discussion based. So it's like a small intimate group of people having discussion, a topic. So I'll have to check and see them if they're into recording those and putting them up. I believe that the last one I went to was recorded, so I'll have to check and see the possibility. That could be super interesting for people\n\nCole Heilborn (24:33): For sure. Well, we're about out of time, but I think my last question for you is where did the name for your company come from? You've got a really great story.", "token_estimate": 264, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Brennah Rosenthal"], "start_timestamp": "23:44", "end_timestamp": "24:33", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "11b7cabd-4c9a-4b9c-816c-afca8d211909", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (24:41): So when I was going to start off on my own, I had an accountant tell me, you can't run under Brenna Rosenthal. It's sketchy. And I was like, okay, that's enough for people don't want using my home address for most things, and if something were to happen, I don't want someone to come knocking down my door and taking everything I own. He was like, you should probably just have a business name. I was like, no, I never thought about that. I thought I was just going to operate under just Brenna, Rosenthal Media or whatever it was. So I took a step back and talked to some friends and they were like, well, someone said to me, they were like, what are the two things that you love most in life? And I was like, video and production and photography. And then climbing. I've been climber for about 15 years, and they're like, okay, so is there a name of a climb that you can think of? (25:30): And I went through and did a bunch and I was like, okay, wait. There's a climb up in Clear Creek Canyon that's just outside of Denver in Golden called the Great Escape. It is an extremely hard and scary climb, and it is one that I go back to try almost every summer since I've lived here in Colorado, which has been awesome. I've been there with handfuls of different people. I've taken my mom up there before and I was like, hold on a second. That actually is a great name. Because that climb is forever what's called Project People in climbing industry call climbs that they're working on a project, and so is this business. This business is a forever project for me. And then the other kind of second meaning to that escape is that I was escaping these full-time jobs that were dragging me down and preventing me from starting this business. So it's kind of got that dual meaning, but mostly comes from that climb in Clear Creek Canyon. That will be a forever project.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:35): I love that. That's fantastic. And what a cool story you can tell potential clients, new clients, or just people who are trying to get to know you. I think that shows a lot about your personality,", "token_estimate": 528, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:41", "end_timestamp": "26:35", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "e3164da9-bb2f-4dcb-ac18-c953f0ff3c37", "episode_id": "a04a9775-c55f-412e-8cb6-7ad9fd829dce", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4831", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Brennah Rosenthal (26:48): And I am thankful that I had a friend that ran me through that. And now with my kind of rebrand, I'm working with one of the guys in my office, Adam Vicar, who's a very talented art director and illustrative artist to go through, and he's kind of brought that story back to life with some of the new rebrand he's doing. So I'm super excited to introduce that to the world as soon as it's ready.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:08): Well, if people want to find you, if they want to follow along with the work that you guys are doing, where can they go?\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (27:14): So two things. So I post my work both on my personal and business accounts, so at Great Escape Media on Instagram, and then Brenna, R-O-B-R-E-N-N-A-H-R-O on Instagram, and then great escape media.com.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:30): Fantastic. Well, Brenna, thanks so much. It's been a pleasure chatting with you.\n\nBrennah Rosenthal (27:34): Yeah, it's been a pleasure. Thanks so much.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:36): Alright, take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of Brand Story. If you enjoyed today's episode, please consider leaving us a review on Apple Podcast or sharing this with a friend or colleague. Tune in next time for another episode. I.", "token_estimate": 311, "speakers": ["Brennah Rosenthal", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:48", "end_timestamp": "27:36", "podcast_index": 16, "episode_name": "Ep 16: Brennah Rosenthal | The Changing Production Landscape | Great Escape Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-16-brennah-rosenthal-the-changing-production-landscape-great-escape-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There is this personal touch you get with these small shops that you might not get from bigger agencies.\"", "podcast_length": "44:38"}, {"id": "c6f2f253-b770-4060-8c1c-bc1372bd427c", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:00): Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'm going to be your host Today Brand story is all about the marketing professionals who are working in the industry today. The landscape is constantly changing and evolving, takes really great smart people to run a successful brand, and that's what this podcast is all about. It's all about the people behind the brands we interact with, the challenges they face, as well as their personal journey. So if you're interested in learning more about what it takes to run a top brand, I hope you'll love this podcast and find something educational about it. Enjoy today's episode. Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Nate Nichols, the founder of the Pallet Group. He's also the co-founder of the Allyship and Action Summit. Nate, how are you, sir?\n\nNate Nichols (00:41): I'm good, man. Been a wonderful, wonderful week so far. Busy and productive, but feeling good and nourished.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:48): Good, I'm glad to hear it. I'm super excited to have you on today. I'm really excited to talk and share a little bit more about your story, but where are you joining us from? You're not in the country?\n\nSpeaker 3 (01:00): No, so my life partner is German and we stepped out around June 6th and have been in Germany since then. It will be through August 13th. And we thought by the time we got back, the virus would be settled down, but apparently not. Apparently American wants me to be a refugee in Germany.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:23): You've probably got it better over there than we do over here.\n\nSpeaker 3 (01:25): It's wild. It's just nothing but nature and just rolling hills and green forest and lots of meditation and gardens for fresh fruity food. I can't complain. It's been a wonderful experience.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:39): So tell us a little bit about the Palate Group.", "token_estimate": 474, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nate Nichols", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "01:39", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "1997cb07-82b6-492f-a590-e98051d8286b", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Speaker 3 (01:41): So Palate Group is a bunch of unfiltered humans. We are a creative agency and production company whose basis is on unfilter imagination. So our role in function in the creatives and advertising spaces to get people to peel back their lens and their filter on what they think their imagination could look like and really shake up where they start imagining from and push them to be a bit more unfiltered and how they approach their ideas and real and honest and full of truth. So whenever I say unfiltered, it's with honesty and with truth. That's what we're looking for. And just leave your bias at home as much as possible, but with the rest of the skeletons and bones in your closet and just create from a space of truth and honesty. And so that's what our claim to fame is. And so we're a creative agency. We produce photography commercial shoots with brands like Footlocker. We produce long form content with brands like Sacks, underwear or short form content on social, we produce experiences. So my partner, she's experiential producer, and so she's produced experience with YouTube and Google and Under Armour. So our clients run the gamut from apparel to retail to we've done elder care in the past. We have a wild, wild story of getting to where we are. But yeah, we're a creative, a agency and production company that we experiences in content.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:13): Very cool. And you guys are based out of New York City, is that right?\n\nSpeaker 3 (03:15): Yes. So we are originally based in Brooklyn, best side, Brooklyn. We founded in Philadelphia and moved to Brooklyn three years ago.", "token_estimate": 405, "speakers": ["Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:41", "end_timestamp": "03:15", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "0831f863-3c0e-4573-a6fa-f505fc15b2ea", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (03:23): I'm super excited. I think this fits really well with our topic today, which is just diversity amongst the creative and advertising industry. And I know you've got, I'm going to let you talk about it because this is something that you can speak really well to and I'm excited to turn it over to you. But it's neat to hear a little bit about your company. I think that fits really well with what I understand about who you are and what drives you. So let's talk about diversity in the advertising industry. Maybe talk us through your experience and what that's looked like.\n\nSpeaker 3 (03:56): Well, I'm going to pause real quick. I'm going to do a remix on your podcast and I'm going to flip it back to you. What's your experience with racism and diversity or a lack there of diversity given your experience in the media industry so far and in the outdoor space? How does it look to you?\n\nCole Heilborn (04:17): That's a great question. And I was thinking about this when we had our phone call a couple weeks ago doing a pre intro and after our call, I was thinking about what is diversity in the industry that I'm, the bubbles that I'm in. And so we do a lot of work in the outdoor industry, a lot of commercial video photo work and all of our clients, at least all the people we've interacted with on the brand side, I would say like 60% and these are rough, are white males. And I guess the other percentage is probably white females. And then on the crew side, on the freelancer side, most of the team that we work with are white males. And there's, so I guess that's my experience. It's predominantly white male.", "token_estimate": 410, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "03:23", "end_timestamp": "04:17", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "a3f9af28-10b4-4d36-b1cb-aa583bb2e701", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Speaker 3 (05:10): And so that's most of the world's experience, right? It is just white men froing around doing as they please. And we're complicit in the issue because we haven't spoken up until George Floyd lost his life a few months ago. And we see it, we just don't want to say anything about it. We're just like, this is life. This is how we exist. We just keep contracting white men and we carved out a little space for women, which is terrible and it's unacceptable. And I pride myself in the communities that I run in and the ethnicities that I surround myself in with because I know my bias, my perception, my lens of the world is limited and I can only be so much of myself if I'm not surrounding myself with people who are going to push me to grow and develop who I am is ever changing and growing and I want to change and grow into a person that's reflective of a beautiful world that accepts every person and every culture and every walk of life. (06:22): And so when we do our work from a creative perspective, you'll see our body of work. It was with a tampon brand named Cora. It was one of the first subscription based tampon brands all organic. Half of the money sold for one of those tampons subscription goes to supporting kids who have their menstrual periods in Africa. And this woman designed a program to support and enable women in Africa who are children, teenagers who miss the opportunity to go to school. These women have fought tooth and nail to go to school, but when they have their period because they don't have menstrual products, they miss out on their education. And so we serve people like that and brands like that. And one of our first products with them was doing nude photography of women with all shapes and sizes and from all walks of life. And so you saw stretch marks, you saw bodies that you wouldn't normally see on an Instagram feed. And so we talk about diversity. It isn't from just a racial perspective, it's from a body shape perspective. It's from an accessibility perspective. It's from having these challenging conversations like you and I just did being like, oh wow, you thought to yourself last week that you surround yourself with nothing but white men. And now we get to have a conversation about it.", "token_estimate": 566, "speakers": ["Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "05:10", "end_timestamp": "05:10", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "a0b7ab67-fe4b-4b0e-b9f9-070261ee66fa", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:47): It's\n\nSpeaker 3 (07:47): Like now it's like, because we know each other, I'm going to hold you accountable. I'm going to look at your work and be like, I wonder if this dude actually did stab some people of color and some black people and understand the role he has now with ensuring your industry and your vertical has more inclusion and more equity in it. Because black people like to travel and go outdoors and people of color like to travel and go outdoors. And we made Timberland what it is. It was to say we can't make brands. We make brands billions of dollars. So open it up. I don't even remember what your question was because I flipped the question back at you.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:31): No, that's all good. So tell us a little bit about your journey. What has your time in the advertising industry looked like? What's your path looked like?", "token_estimate": 219, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "07:47", "end_timestamp": "08:31", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "1c9b9f8a-2820-4889-84dd-d1595dfb46da", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Speaker 3 (08:41): Man, I think my journey begun really when I was a teenager. I started doing design at my computer and my brother had to built a gateway computer. He had downloaded Kim. And I just started designing and just started designing shit for my MySpace when I was a 16-year-old because honestly, I never felt comfortable outside of my home where I grew up. I never felt comfortable inside of my home. I grew up not knowing my father. My mother had a mental disability and so her and I just didn't get along. So I would just block her out by playing little Wayne Carter three and just design my MySpace and just trick it out. And outside in the streets, my friends, I just didn't want to do the things my friends were doing. I wanted to play football. I didn't want to be just hanging out on the streets, just hanging out on the streets doing nothing. (09:39): And so I chose to be in front of the computer and design and that afforded me a skillset and afforded me the opportunity to be able to have language to meet people and be like, oh wow, you have a clothing line, you have a boutique. Cool, can I hang out with you? And I wound up being my first mentor when I was 18 or 17, excuse me. And he was a graphic designer and had tattoos on his arm. He had his hair locked up like mine. And he just living a lifestyle that I just respected and admired. He was a creative, he was self-expressed, and that's all I wanted. And so that was my start in the creative industry. And I knew that I was hooked because this guy was an adult. I knew I didn't want to be a clothing line producer and screenprinting shirts and going out to trade shows in Vegas or New York and buying and selling and wholesaling deals. (10:38): I just wasn't my thing. But I knew I wanted to be a designer and a creative. And so I moved to Philadelphia. I went to the Art Institute of Philadelphia to study graphic design. The whole time I just fucking hustled client after client. By the time I graduated, I had 300 clients and from print to web dev to design. And it was pretty just a very, it was a mind bending experience knowing that the world was limitless within reason. Meaning as soon as I got out of my house in Connecticut, I could do whatever I want and sell whatever I want and save whatever I want and spend wherever I want, go wherever I wanted. But I still wasn't successful by any means. I still was living a very free life, but didn't have any traction. And it was hard because although I was hustling garing these clients, I still couldn't really afford to pay rent. (11:43): I still couldn't afford to have healthy meals. So I'm in college buying a dollar a pack of pasta and a dollar can of tomato sauce, and it's up to me. And at one point in my college career, I wound up living in a warehouse, a 300,000 square foot warehouse with a mattress on the floor and it was a plastic sheep between the mattress and the floor. And I'm interviewing for a contract with Hyundai while sleeping on this mattress. It was a $5 million campaign with Hyundai and interviewing to be a social media manager on the account with a graphic design degree. (12:25): Literally just taking my life into my own hands, but also I actually didn't have control. What, I'm a kid who takes a shower at a gym every morning and our roache is walking by my bed. And so my starting advertising industry was sort of fucked up because I didn't have the privilege of someone supporting me through college and making sure I felt like I had a workspace at home and food so I could just focus on my studies. I got a B average maybe, and everyone disrespected my hustle. So I hustled my way into advertising. And that was sort of the start was on a mattress in a 300,000 square foot warehouse managing social media for Hyundai.", "token_estimate": 947, "speakers": ["Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "08:41", "end_timestamp": "08:41", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "a5440f2a-8128-49a9-8674-0f55e8e96504", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:18): So then walk us from post-college to where you're at now. This is kind of the next leg or next, I guess there's a few different legs in your journey in this time period, so keep it going.\n\nSpeaker 3 (13:30): My experience on the Hyundai campaign was just mind. It was another level of mind altering because brands are just pouring all this money into finding pockets of culture. They can blend their products in their brand ethos too, to get consumers to enjoy it. And it was a new experience for me to see how brand identity and creative was being interlaced in culture. And so this is where my brand really tried to stick because I'm all about community and finding the right people who reflect to the same values that I do sort of grow with. And I saw that in brands for the first time. I'm like, oh, cool. So as a brand, your vision is to develop a product that fits into a pocket of culture and community and activate your community around that product brand. And it showed up in this campaign as me running this Twitter with a certain tone and then them throwing parties at Chelsea Market in New York with three cars pulled up inside Jeff Staple from Staple Designs doing Ted Talks. (14:44): You had rappers coming through and performing ASAP Rockies and all the creative for the videos and all this really fly shit. And I'm like, wow, this is dope. I could do this the rest of my life. And so that's where I found my clear vision on I want to be creating this content. My vision design was cute and it was able for me to, it was the foundation for me to get in the industry, but where I really want to exist is creating content to really influence and inspire culture and society. And so as I was figuring myself out, understanding my personal values, I was able to shape my values into how I wanted to design an agency and develop how the agency functioned for our clients. And so after that project, I should say I want up meeting these two dudes who owned an agency and while still living in the warehouse, I sort of helped them scale their agency to a quarter million dollars in revenue in a year and a half. (15:55): And at this point, I'm still living in a warehouse. I'm fat just all over the place, but I'm still just happy. I was never sad walking through that warehouse. I've dated women. We were on the warehouse roof. They thought it was cool in my mind, I wasn't homeless, I was just living this cool life. I do what I want. I want to go jog for an hour. I'm going to go take up the room over there. It's 8,000 square feet and run for an hour and wish, listen to Wash the Throne, JayZ and Kanye. That's just my life, whatever. And so after a while though, you start to see and feel that the life I'm designing really isn't something that I'm proud of and I'm happy with because as I'm learning myself, I'm learning that I like nicer things and I don't have access to things. (16:49): And I'm like, well, I'm busting my ass. I deserve access to things. I'm working 10 times harder than most of my peers. Why don't I have a nice apartment? Why don't I have healthy food? But I not have a sense of self that I am truly proud of. And I noticed that inside of the agency I was working before as well. I was a partner, but I still was just kind of figuring myself out. And I wound up getting to this point where this agency isn't a reflection of me. I don't know who I am, but I feel like I'm growing into myself. And I just had this aha moment and I decided to found Pallet Group and Pallet Group. Is this a pallet, an art palette is this platform for creativity. Artists put acrylic paint or oil paint on the palette and then they create a painting with it on Canvas. (17:50): And so for me, palate Group was this thing of owning that I am a facilitator and the enabler of other creatives. My thing is I want to represent a creative community and help facilitate their creative savagery. They're enabling of what they want to produce. And that's when I found a pallet group in 2015. And from there I was going to walk away from the other company and the gentleman that was a partner at the time with me said, Nate, you built this company. Why don't you take it from me? Buy it. And so I bought the company and it was to start a pallet group. So since then we've done projects like the one Quora I just spoke of. We've done projects where we've activated hundreds of people to work out in a city and we're able to measure their calories burned and interesting things like NFL players on that campaign. We don't work with Chevrolet, don't work with a bunch of dope brands nationally. And so we just want to carve a nice unfiltered niche in the market. And it's just, that's representative of our core values. And that's sort of the brand that we're developing. That was 7, 6, 7 years ago. And now we're in COVID and still working with brands like Footlocker and producing a bunch of cool virtual events and experiences, and that's where we are today.", "token_estimate": 1247, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "13:18", "end_timestamp": "13:30", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "f21612af-890b-485f-af09-c3f70c95a35d", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (19:25): How has your race presented challenges for you throughout your journey from college or even pre-college to where you are now?\n\nSpeaker 3 (19:35): Being a black person, it takes you a while to really understand how you function in society as an African-American. Unless someone tells you, you sort of just walk around and just think that you're someone else. Until you hear someone drop the N bomb in a very derogatory way to towards you, or you see that you're working 10 times better as a white peer and for whatever reason you still don't get the promotion. And for me, it's just my mother was never healthy mentally or financially don't know my father. And no one in my family really instilled a certain level of financial literacy in me and I didn't have time. How am I going to have time to learn financial literacy as I'm trying to hustle three times harder as someone else while also studying college to understand that this loan with a 15% interest rate isn't a smart move, but my college professor or financial aid people are like, Hey, you need this, you loan if you want to stay in college and just like, shit, I need this. (21:05): I guess I need this $8,000 loan, 10% interest rate. And then 10 years later here I am stuck with this $20,000 bill that I don't know when I'm going to be able to pay off interest rate's. Insane. And it's like I have five more of those. I five more of those loans. So that means my student loan debt is over a hundred thousand bucks for a three year program. And that's just a part of the issue is the idea that you are supposed to just know. And that's so sad because I don't know, did your parents sit you down? How was your college education set up?\n\nCole Heilborn (21:47): Yeah, so I did running start when I was in high school and I was able to get a couple years of college done. And then when I decided to go to four year university, my parents were fortunate enough to be able to pay for part of my college. And so we worked out some split where I paid for a part and they paid for a part.", "token_estimate": 516, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "19:25", "end_timestamp": "21:47", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "12028797-f48f-4f0d-9543-8d8fadf6d50d", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Speaker 3 (22:10): So that's sort of like the expectation if you're growing up and is that your parents will help you, your parents will help you, your parents will help you. And a lot of people, black people don't have the support of their parents because we just had systemic racism throughout history happen in our communities like crack, heroin happening in the seventies, redlining in housing issues and in the sixties in certain communities. And there's just so many other challenges that really mess with the trajectory of young black people in America. And so it happens all the time. And that is just one example I'll share that is tangible. Yeah, to answer your question,\n\nCole Heilborn (23:05): Yeah, George Floyd passed away recently. How did that make you feel and what did you do with that energy and all of those feelings? Because I think what you're working on is really incredible and I'd love for you to share.", "token_estimate": 232, "speakers": ["Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:10", "end_timestamp": "23:05", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "792c7e19-a202-4dc9-95f6-4d77f4deca3c", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Speaker 3 (23:20): So George Floyd passing was just a very sad experience. It still is a very sad experience because there's just so many people that have passed away, black people that have passed away, thousands of black bodies that have been just through time just passed through just another dead body. And so there's a book Anahi coach wrote, and it's called Between the World and Me, and he basically shares with his son through this book that you are a black body and beware this world does not make for you. And when George Floyd died, Floyd died, it was murdered, I should say. (24:28): It was just sad and angry. I was angry. And that's how everyone feels felt, I'm be honest. It's like you shouldn't ask any black person how they felt about that because it's just like, it's obvious that we're just angry. It keeps happening and just needs to change. We need everyone to just get the fuck up and change and help change because there's no question racism is real. It's whether you're going to be anti-racist or do nothing and be complicit, you doing nothing speaks to volumes as many volumes and decibels as someone just being overtly racist. And so my life partner and I, after crying together one morning, we decided that we were going to do something about it, and we decided to produce a virtual event. And this virtual event was something we thought of on June 2nd with some collaborators. And we launched it on June 12th maybe, and it was on June 18th. (26:02): And the vision for this virtual event was how do we give the advertising industry who just posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all these statements on how they all of a sudden care about black people? And then between the four walls and in society, how do we see it for telling the truth? How do we build a forum for people to come together and truly, truly speak to what they're feeling? We know how black people are feeling. It's fucking, we're angry and we're sad, but what are white leaders really feeling? Do their feelings match up with their company statements? And we decided to do a forum that speaks to that. And so what we decided was to do a three hour long virtual summit and it brought out 2,500 humans in the seven days that we promoted it. And we had industry leaders, CEO of TBWA, Shiat, the founder of David and Goliath, chief marketing officer of Microsoft Gaming. (27:21): We had a CMO of translation, charleser Barnes, Kai Lawson from WPP. We had employees from Adidas. We just had amazing human beings speak. And it was like the industry paused for the three hours we were doing the summit to be present to really understand their role in anti-racism. And that was the vision for allyship in action was develop a virtual conference that really, really, really cut the shit and unfiltered allyship in action in the advertising industry. And so we are committed to continuing to doing those summits over the next, who knows how long. We're just going to continue", "token_estimate": 744, "speakers": ["Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "23:20", "end_timestamp": "23:20", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "9e982a24-1d10-4ba4-83b7-a16e48352595", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:09): So people can log in. It's a virtual event and the goal is just to provide education. And I guess what does it look like more tangibly? You have these amazing speakers, what do you talk about?\n\nSpeaker 3 (28:20): So in the opening of Allyship and Action Summit, I say, and it's still available to watch on YouTube. I say, because you're here today, does not dissolve you from being a problem in racism, in systemic racism and oppression. Your goal today in understanding allyship is to identify your role. You're being productive with shifting and reversing the problem. And so the goal is for people to hear from white leaders their vision on how they plan on combating this so they can go back to their organizations and provide feedback. The goal is to listen to black people and people of color who have been doing the work for years to learn and listen on how they could be productive and proactive with systemic racism in fighting that. We also designed workshops, and so we have workshops on unpacking race bias or your feelings about race. We had workshops for black employees to just have a space to grieve and talk through their emotions. We had a workshop around marching daily, so what does that look like if you're marching daily and how does that experience feel for you in just a bunch of different like that. And then we just had fireside chats on racism and protests and work culture, workplace culture. So stuff like that is what we've talked through.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:53): So I know this question might be an obvious, your answer might be an obvious one, but I'll ask it anyways, whether it's through Allyship in Action or some other programs, what's your hope for the advertising industry in the future?", "token_estimate": 437, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "28:09", "end_timestamp": "29:53", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "f4560020-1f4d-4f94-8889-15fab32dab67", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Speaker 3 (30:05): The hope is that honestly, I think white men and white leaders need to be center themselves. So many white men are like, what can I do? What can I do? What can I do? And it's just like, honestly, bro, this ain't about you anymore. You got us here. This is time for you to enable other people and make it a very fluid and just easy process to let the people, black people and people of color, step in and do the work that they've always been able to do, but never had the opportunity to do. And that's at the C-suite level, that's at the creative level, that's a design level, strategic level. It's just like, that's what I want to see on the other side of this. It's just people get out of the way because that's the only way this will really change is people just decentering themselves. White people needed to center themselves. This isn't about out you. And I think the steps we're taking now will ultimately get there. Like being transparent, transparent with your workforce, diversity, sharing where you are with your workforce, diversity, reading and learning and growing about racism and around white fragility. These are nice steps, but ultimately the goal is to just fucking white people just get out of the way because specifically white men, fuck this up, man. (31:53): Yeah, fuck this up. And that is the end goal for sure. But the steps are reconditioning learning and growing and people doing their own diligence, designing programs that enable more comfortable and less alienating cultures. There's an Instagram count called Black at Nike that has 8,000 followers right now over the past 15 days that share black people and people of color's, experiences with racism and discrimination, and just keeps growing and growing. That's just one corporation who's just not safe. Not safe. People don't feel comfortable being themselves there. That's not cool. You should be able to walk around beating yourself all the time. And if people can't feel self-expressed in our industry, what the fuck is our industry?\n\nCole Heilborn (32:50): Yeah, it's all about self-expression.\n\nSpeaker 3 (32:52): Exactly. So that's what we need to change.", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:05", "end_timestamp": "32:52", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "6d51500f-4d5e-447b-b78b-42da9f9aca98", "episode_id": "142406e2-bdcf-4c91-bc3f-b8d708d310db", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4834", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:58): We're about out of time for today, but I really want to thank you for coming on and talking and just sharing your perspective and your experience. If people want to learn more about the Allyship and Action Summit, where can they go? I know you have events coming up in the future that people can join and be a part of. Where can folks find more information?\n\nSpeaker 3 (33:21): So they can go to allyship in action.com to learn about our certification tool that we're building, and if they want to learn more about our events, freelancer cyber summit.com. And our next event is August 4th, and I'm assuming you'll have show notes that we can drop those in and yeah, August 4th is when the next summit is, and we'll be launching that live this coming Monday.\n\nCole Heilborn (33:44): Okay, perfect. So if you want to learn more, take a look at the show notes to find a couple links. But Nate, thank you so much.\n\nNate Nichols (33:51): Beautiful, man. Thank you.\n\nCole Heilborn (33:53): All right, well take care and have a good rest of your day and best of luck on the next virtual summit. I'll see you there. I'll be in the crowd.\n\nNate Nichols (34:00): Appreciate it.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:01): Alright, take care.\n\nNate Nichols (34:02): You too.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:03): Thank you for listening to this episode of Brand Story. If you enjoyed today's episode, please consider leaving us a review on Apple Podcast or sharing this with a friend or colleague. Tune in next time for another episode.", "token_estimate": 379, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3", "Nate Nichols"], "start_timestamp": "32:58", "end_timestamp": "34:03", "podcast_index": 17, "episode_name": "Ep 17: Nate Nichols | Racism and the Ad Industry | Palette Grou‪p", "slug": "ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-17-nate-nichols-racism-and-the-ad-industry-palette-group", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"How do we get the advertising industry - when they posted a bunch of black tiles on Instagram and did all of these statements, how do we build a forum for people to speak what they're feeling.\"", "podcast_length": "44:39"}, {"id": "b7bd9dc4-4e59-44e1-9cc8-8bfda65a488f", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:00): Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'm going to be your host Today Brand story is all about the marketing professionals who are working in the industry today. The landscape is constantly changing and evolving, takes really great smart people to run a successful brand, and that's what this podcast is all about. It's all about the people behind the brands we interact with, the challenges they face, as well as their personal journey. So if you're interested in learning more about what it takes to run a top brand, I hope you'll love this podcast and find something educational about it. Enjoy today's episode. Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Ur. Today I'm sitting down with Lisa Slagel, the founder of Wheelie. Lisa, how are you doing?\n\nLisa Slagle (00:41): I am doing great. Thanks for having me on here today.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:44): Yeah, thanks for joining us. Where are you calling in from today?\n\nLisa Slagle (00:48): I am currently at my house. I left the office, so I'm out. I live in the woods in Whitefish, Montana.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:56): Awesome. So tell us a little bit about wheelie. Who are you guys and what do you do?\n\nLisa Slagle (01:00): So WHEELIE is a creative agency for people who thrive outside. We've been around for 11 years and we work in outdoor industry primarily. A lot of times we joke that every adventure starts with coffee and ends in beer. So we've moved into those verticals pretty comfortably, and we do tons of strategy, creative direction, and a lot of production as well. So full service, we do everything except media buying, never doing that again. And yeah, it's been a wild ride for the last decade of my life.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:39): Yeah, no kidding. I can only imagine. Who are some of the clients that you guys have worked with?\n\nLisa Slagle (01:44): We have an amazing roster of clients ranging from the north face to 10 Barrel. Polar Tech is a big one, and then locally Whitefish Credit Union is amazing to work with. So they've been great.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:58): Nice, nice. And is there anything that you guys have done that people might recognize in any of the commercial work you guys have done?", "token_estimate": 550, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Lisa Slagle"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "01:58", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "2969e715-68c8-4f58-8601-4d759f87a013", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Lisa Slagle (02:05): Yep. We have worked a ton in Women's Bike, so we have done lots and lots of different photos and videos and branding in women's bike as well as, we did a Super Bowl commercial up here recently where we built a 30 foot road gap, and then we had a horse tow in Pro Ski or Park in Costa where he did a back flip and right in front of Pole Bridge in front of the mercantile. We live up by Glacier National Park, so a lot of people are probably familiar with Pole Bridge and kind of how special that place is. So that was a big deal.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:44): Nice. Well, that's awesome. So I am hoping we can spend our time today chatting a little bit about your guys' philosophy towards business and your love of the process. And so I'm hoping you can kind of share a little bit about what makes you guys unique and where did this, I guess, passionate for your business come from in how you do business?", "token_estimate": 230, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:05", "end_timestamp": "02:44", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "36b183af-509d-4848-8ea7-1a2f3da36cd8", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Lisa Slagle (03:04): Yeah, so that's a great question. Yeah, like many agency owners, I am kind of an accidental entrepreneur, meaning I had a design degree and I was snowboarding my face off every day. I was handing out business cards on chair lifts and working at night, and I just loved what I was doing as a designer. And I loved working with small business owners in Crested Butte and saying, what's your idea? Here's my idea. And watching things come to life, that was such a fun, exciting thing to do that involved design and it involved creative thinking. And I just loved the work and got busier and busier and busier and busier. And then just, I was 22 when I started the company 11 years ago. (03:56): So I got busier and started. I just kind of put a bandaid on it and started hiring people to help me. And that's how the agency was born. I just was like, oh, I have too much work. Let me hire someone to help with web or ah. And then I was like, oh wow, I just created a business. Whoops. And that's a pretty common story I think for entrepreneurs, especially agency owners, but it started with this love of being outside and this love of connecting with people and sharing that intrinsic interconnectedness of all things. And then it grew from there. But we haven't changed as people. Well, I mean, complacency is death. So hopefully we've changed, but our ethos haven't changed in that we still love being outside and we love true, true deep connections with people and deep connections with ourselves and the world around us. So that hasn't changed. But then all my dirtbag friends that I met just in life in the ski industry and snowboard industry started getting hired into marketing manager positions. And everything has just grown really organically based upon a very authentic lifestyle. So it's been very positive and quite easy to stay true to our core belief that the outdoors are for everyone and misadventure is just as important as adventure.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:20): I guess that is the definition of adventure. Things got to go wrong for it to be an adventure.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:04", "end_timestamp": "05:20", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "3cbcffe8-c991-4c89-b21c-9711f975a666", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Lisa Slagle (05:24): Yeah, and a lot of those outdoor skills give you business skills too. They're kind of the same thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:34): So you guys really value the human connection to the work that you do. How does that shape what you guys do?\n\nLisa Slagle (05:45): Yeah, human connection. It very much shapes the way we approach work where we, especially in video, which we do tons of, but it's an opportunity to make someone smile. It's an opportunity to create work that resonates with a person or in simpler terms, the videos we make need to make people feel feelings. And if you watch a video and you are kind of like, hmm, you move on with your life, it's not really notable, it's not memorable, you're not really feeling any feelings. That's not the type of creative work that we strive for. We strive for something that makes where someone's like or whoa, whether the vibe is being astounded or feeling emotional or feeling connected. So I think that having that really human-centered approach is the core and foundation of what we do.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:42): How does that creative, that desire to create something that makes someone react, how does that mix with the business goal and how do you make sure that those are working together, but also you're not sacrificing the creative?\n\nLisa Slagle (06:58): I mean, I think beautiful creative work drives sales. Am I allowed to swear on your podcast?\n\nCole Heilborn (07:06): Go for it.", "token_estimate": 364, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:24", "end_timestamp": "07:06", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "ec466532-5b60-4612-8519-4b98a9d9bc73", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Lisa Slagle (07:07): I don't really know. But shitty creative work or mundane creative work doesn't drive sales. And that's the power of creativity. That's the power of why graphic design solves problems. Fine art raises questions. Graphic design solves problems. Really, really good work will connect with humans. And if humans feel connected to something, then that brand becomes part of their story, and then they're buying into the brand and the ethos and products become more like souvenirs. And it's just this really beautiful, effortless thing. And if you can make it look easy, it becomes easy. And so I think creative work is incredibly powerful. I also think it's important to acknowledge that all people, every single human experiences everything in a unique way. And so what one person might experience when watching a video is totally different than what someone else might experience. And just acknowledging that and being able to honor everyone's different experiences and try to hopefully create something with a positive message that's more positive than negative for everyone, obviously.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:12): So when it comes to the work you guys, do you, I guess, what's your mindset? You're obviously very focused on the end deliverable, but I know you guys also really love the process of making the project. How do you navigate", "token_estimate": 338, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:07", "end_timestamp": "08:12", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "e7861ca0-a7d9-4e8c-881d-ea94a83895b1", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Lisa Slagle (08:26): That? Yeah, so I mean, it's different for every single project. Everything we do is so custom that every approach is going to be different. Commonalities between projects in our approach would be more starting with, okay, that's the end goal. How are we going to get there? Is going to be different. But the process itself always remains. We all love making things. I'm the creative director, and so I still love having my hands in all the projects and really, really finding joy and being able to solve those problems. And so the process for me isn't just positive. It's not like, oh, it's going to be a beautiful sunny day and the shoot's going to be perfect. It's more like, well, do I love the problems of the process as well? And so do I love it when it's cloudy and I need to figure out how to make something look summery or it's loving the problems of that process as much as loving those really high dopamine stoke moments as well. And that's this well-rounded process. And if that is something that's worth struggling and enjoying and all sides of human feelings around that process, then it's worth it. And so our crew is pretty hard charging in terms of everybody brings a lot of passion to the table, a lot of different backgrounds. And I mean, I can't speak for all of my employees or I can't speak for any of them. They can speak for themselves, but I think they probably love the process just as much as I do.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:01): Yeah, that's something that I feel like a lot of people in the creative industry talk about is loving the process though. From your perspective, why is that important? Why should people focus on that rather than the end goal?", "token_estimate": 424, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:26", "end_timestamp": "10:01", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "80c7c01e-0c1e-4f57-85f9-78dfcbeff718", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Lisa Slagle (10:13): Yeah, because focusing on the end goal isn't enough. That's just because it's simply a goal. And so if you are trying to break into marketing and you're having a vision of this is what my life will be like down the road, I'll be on these, I always use the example of why people want to work in the ski industry. Like, oh, I'll be going on heli trips with pro athletes. If that's the vision that you have, that's great and beautiful, but it's not enough to sustain you through the problems of becoming outdoor marketing in the ski industry. If you don't like hiking with a super heavy backpack and that's part of your job, or if you've ever been on a shoot where everyone else is skiing and you're standing there freezing your ass off and all your lenses are messed up and just sort of these problems that you have to solve in the field, if you don't love that process, that's okay. Maybe it's time to pivot the end goal. And so it's good to have goals, but it's also really important to notice if you actually like the process. (11:29): And for me, I can relate that to snowmobiling. I bought a snowmobile many years ago. I still have my snowmobile, and I envisioned myself loving snowmobiling. And people kept saying to me, oh, it's like, what happens to every snowmobiler? You're just going to stop taking your snowboard one day and then you're just going to be snowmobiling. And I'm like, no, I don't think so. I bought mine for access, but in my head I was like, yeah, that's really in my head. I was b rapping around having a great time without a snowboard, just loving snowmobiling. And then it turns out through hours and hours on a sled. I don't really love snowmobiling, I still love snowboarding. And so for me, I was able to shift that goal of like, no, I don't want to be a snowmobile, or I still just want to be a snowboarder who owns a snowmobile. And being able to, that was one where the end goal was cooler than the actual process.", "token_estimate": 493, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle"], "start_timestamp": "10:13", "end_timestamp": "10:13", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "b6abf086-774f-46ee-9333-d888fd1df7e3", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:24): Let's switch gears a little bit. I'm curious, as a business owner, someone who's been at it for a while, how have you stuck to your core values and have there been times during your journey where you felt yourself veering off? And how have you had to course correct and get back on track?\n\nLisa Slagle (12:38): Yeah, I mean, I think it's really interesting because sticking to your core values is really easy. If it's just you and you're the one guiding the conversations, you're the one guiding the work. It definitely, when you open up to collaboration, it's like, okay, do our core values as a company, are they inclusive enough for everyone at this company? Are they inclusive enough for everyone that we work with? And then it becomes so much more about me as an individual and more about wheelie as a brand. And so for me, sticking to my core values really has been interesting. And because I started the business when I was 22, a 22-year-old punk kid with a snowboard, and now I am a woman, I find more depth in certain things than I used to be honest, I'm not nearly as shallow and ridiculous as I was when I was 22. I've evolved a lot as a person, and so being able to not identify with, I have a pretty irreverent brand. Wheelie is kind of, we throw down, we charge hard, everybody's sending things. It's a pretty irreverent brand. I'm really proud of it. Also though, for me, there's this huge side of me as a woman, I'm soft and gentle and thoughtful and super, super introverted. And having that disconnect has been a really interesting thing as I've totally grown up to not grow out of it.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:13): So as you've grown the company from just yourself to this awesome team of people, how do you process translating those core values to other people, but also incorporating other people's opinions and values into the business now?", "token_estimate": 470, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Lisa Slagle"], "start_timestamp": "12:24", "end_timestamp": "14:13", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "8e4c5e37-9cc6-4c95-b120-9af358d95b29", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Lisa Slagle (14:29): Yeah, I mean, I think thinking about it in a framing of a good fit rather than me pushing my values on someone, but is it a good fit? Is this person going to be happy working at my agency? Are we going to be happy integrating them into our team? Is it a good fit? And then doing that as well with clients, is this a good fit? Are we a good fit for your style tone, voice level of knowledge, level of expertise? And then obviously we don't want to work with brands and companies that deviate largely from our values, so we just wouldn't do that. That's incredibly important to the agency and making sure we're all just in full alignment and having conversations about why things may or may not be a good fit keeps it pretty objective and keeps us staying on course to who we are and true to what we believe in and what we work toward. And it feels pretty effortless in that regard.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:34): So along the way, I'm curious, what's been one of your biggest challenges? Maybe it's internally, externally, what have you had to overcome that's been the largest obstacle on your personal journey?", "token_estimate": 281, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "14:29", "end_timestamp": "15:34", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "587c9a14-4e2b-4dea-b699-ebe739f43001", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Lisa Slagle (15:49): I mean, there are many, right? Because everyone is always evolving as a person, and I'm constantly evolving. So there's that internal growth, obviously that carries everyone through their lives, which that's been an interesting journey in terms of being a creative director, but then also becoming a boss and then realizing people really need career paths and they need leadership. And sometimes that's more important than being in the weeds. It's more important to lead the team rather than do the really fun projects. So that's always an internal, I always joke that every job is the best job at wheelie. I'm like, oh, man, you get to work on that. That's the best. So that's an interesting thing to navigate internally. And then obviously externally, it's being young, being a woman, navigating those conversations, negotiating, learning how to stand up for your worth, stand up for your beliefs, things that you don't learn in art school, and then also sharing that and becoming a mentor to others is really important to me. But yeah, that's been sometimes a pretty gnarly ride, for sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:13): So if you could look back in 10 years or 20 years from now and you could see the path that wheelie has blazed, what would make you proud and what would make you really excited about the work that the company has done? Or I guess the impact that you've left along the way\n\nLisa Slagle (17:32): 20 years from now? What's my hope for the company? Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (17:35): Sure.", "token_estimate": 380, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:49", "end_timestamp": "17:35", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "8a3ee49e-abf6-4a91-9409-5c846b27b61f", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Lisa Slagle (17:38): I think my hope for wheelie is that we continue to elevate women in the outdoors as well as create work. We always say market what matters. And so to use marketing for a greater good, not to just put a bunch more plastic in the ocean, but actually have a positive impact on a circular economy. And just trying to use marketing to better the world, because being able to communicate is such a strong skillset and being able to make creative work that hopefully helps our planet and helps our people not to sound totally self-righteous. We have all the answers here at this great creative agency, but hopefully we contribute to society in a positive way, and hopefully we help put on workshops for photography to help get more perspective behind the lens, which is just as important as who's in front of the lens. And so hopefully impacting a lot of people on the personal level as well as on that B2B level.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:38): Yeah, I think that's great. I think all of us creatives are kind of on our own journey, but we're also kind of in the same boat together in a way. For people who are somewhere along their own journey and they're trying to figure out how to build a sustainable business that doesn't deviate from their own personal values, what would you say to someone who's trying to just sort through those conversations from someone who's gone through it themselves?\n\nLisa Slagle (19:08): I would say it's just as important to say no to things that are a bad fit as it is to say yes to things that are, and making sure that sometimes, well, obviously what you do is important, but sometimes what you don't do is just as important. And so if you don't stand for something, then you stand for nothing and you don't want to stand for nothing. So making sure that understanding that not taking a stand is just as powerful as taking a stand.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:43): Yeah, no, we're really well said. Well, I think we're just about out of time for today, but if people want to follow along with the work that you are doing, I know you've got a bunch of different platforms, blogs, podcasts, excuse me. Where can people tune in?", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:38", "end_timestamp": "19:43", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "88a8bcd0-18e2-470a-97ff-f1a4873eb0b6", "episode_id": "3c73eb7f-7835-4fc2-a60f-d07364aeba4a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4835", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Lisa Slagle (19:58): I think probably the best place is just wheelie creative.com or on Instagram at Wheelie Creative, and that's where you can find links to our podcasts and workshops and leadership training programs and all of that.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:14): Okay, great. Well, Lisa, thank you so much for being on the podcast. It's been great chatting, and thanks for sharing your brief snapshot of your company and your guys' work that you're doing. It's great to be able to share it.\n\nLisa Slagle (20:27): Absolutely. Yeah. Thanks for having me.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:29): Alright, take care.\n\nLisa Slagle (20:31): Cool, thanks.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:32): Thank you for listening to this episode of Brand Story. If you enjoyed today's episode, please consider leaving us a review on Apple Podcast or sharing this with a friend or colleague. Tune in next time for another episode.", "token_estimate": 219, "speakers": ["Lisa Slagle", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:58", "end_timestamp": "20:32", "podcast_index": 18, "episode_name": "Ep 18: Lisa Slagle | Loving the Process | Wheeli‪e‬", "slug": "ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-18-lisa-slagle-loving-the-process-wheelie", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think beautiful creative work drives sales\"", "podcast_length": "44:40"}, {"id": "36cb9799-60b8-4d71-8a17-88d029765ae2", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:02): Welcome to Brand Story, the podcast where we interview individuals who are creating and designing the brands we interact with every day. My name is Cole Hilborn, and today I'm here with Brian Seals with Ketchup and Mustard, a design and content marketing agency based out of Bellingham, Washington. Brian, how are you?\n\nBrian Seales (00:15): I'm great, thanks Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:16): Yeah, thank you for being here. So can you tell me a little bit about what you guys do?\n\nBrian Seales (00:21): I think you kind of distilled it really well in the introduction. We're mostly a graphic design company that handles a lot of content strategy and branding for our clients as they need it. We tell a lot of stories through branding with visual elements, but we also do a lot of copywriting as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:38): So what does that look like? Without going into too much detail, what does that look like in the day-to-day for you?\n\nBrian Seales (00:45): Well, for the day-to-day, it's mostly conceptualizing visual elements on screen throughout the day and then interacting with clients at the beginning of the end of the week. But each client has a completely different need, and so we try to evaluate where we're going to be best fit in their organization, and if we need to do copywriting for them and they have a design portion onsite, they can take care of that. And if they don't, we jump in there as well. So our services are really tailored to individual client needs more than just a packaged product.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:16): Gotcha. So you don't offer one set options for your clients, you kind of build it as you go?\n\nBrian Seales (01:21): Yeah, we haven't niched ourselves into any type of industry, so as you can imagine, retail is a lot different than a consultant or service organization. And so their needs are different depending on what they're doing for their consumers or their business to business relationships. And they're very different from one to the client to the next.", "token_estimate": 507, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Brian Seales"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:21", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "2e938944-6df5-4167-a1dd-bb9b3f45e156", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:44): Who are some brands that you guys have worked with? You guys are based here in Bellingham. Do you work with brands primarily here? Kind of regionally,\n\nBrian Seales (01:51): Actually, nationally and globally. One of our bigger brands is Trillium Dell, which is based in Galesburg, Illinois. We helped them with their branding project last year. We have a brewery in Mount Vernon that's opening in the summer district brewing a thousand acres. Cider House downtown is going to be opening here pretty soon. We help them with their branding from start to finish, and we do help with a lot of the ongoing branding evolutions for existing brands where we're not necessarily doing new branding work, but we're evolving it into the next level. And we worked with downtown Bellingham partnership a lot in the past couple of years to help identify needs within Bellingham and through them made a lot of relationships with local brands to help elevate their branding.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:37): Very cool. Very cool. So if you had to describe what a brand is, it's kind of an elusive concept, it's a thing, but how would you describe it?", "token_estimate": 287, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Brian Seales"], "start_timestamp": "01:44", "end_timestamp": "02:37", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "388ce644-b245-413f-b126-c3e8da5214ab", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Brian Seales (02:46): Well, I would say every individual in the world has their own brand, whether they know it or not, and you can identify someone by their voice and their personality and that the way they carry themselves and applying that same philosophy to a company and determining how they project themselves to their audience is really the branding concept behind every organization. And so establishing a strong brand for a company allows for scalability and consistency in the marketplace for years to come. If you think about one person owning a business and they have a branding strategy, it's a pretty good chance that they're not going to deviate from that very much. It's going to be exactly what it is, and it may actually be a representation of them as individuals, but when you get into larger organizations of 10 to a hundred to 500 employees, you want to make sure that the voice and the personality is carried through consistently everywhere. And so creating a brand toolkit that establishes what the personality of this company is ahead of time will help keep it consistent for years to come. No matter who you hand this off to, they'll be able to just roll with it. (03:53): And so yeah, it's really just this at a glance perspective of what a company actually is.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:58): It sounds like what you're describing is kind of building a foundation of what the company is, why they do what they do, who they are, why are those things necessary to have in place and for the CEO or the guy who's cleaning the shop floor, why is that important for them to all understand as they're trying to grow their business?", "token_estimate": 411, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:46", "end_timestamp": "03:58", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "52941f50-7ac5-4dc6-be6f-bcda006f786a", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Brian Seales (04:19): Well, most of the branding concepts that we create for our clients are based on a very specific target audience. So when you have someone in an organization that wants to deviate from that brand, they're essentially deviating from that target audience as well. And so having that brand toolkit available to them and says, you're not a lumberjack. I know you want to be, but you're not. We're actually catering to the road cycler crowd. That is a really important statement to make in that branding concept. It's really important to keep that very consistent.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:55): I see. So would you say that it's hard to identify who your audience is, who your buyer is if you don't know what your brand is? And\n\nBrian Seales (05:03): It can go either way. Your brand can create an audience for sure. If you're a new industry or what I would consider an emerging market, you could create a brand that is based on no audience whatsoever and have pretty decent success. You can kind of create that. If you are a disruptive market and you're taking advantage of some other audiences within, you can take it. We own the tap trail in town, so we're involved with all of the breweries in one way or another. And so that's a disruptive market when a new brewery comes into town and their audience is already predetermined for them. And so you want to lean on some of the conventions and that audience to be more successful early on and maybe evolve it later. But in most cases, disruptive markets work well with an existing audience. So if you came in and you said, I want to start this business and it's doing this, this, and I'll look at it and say, okay, this has been done a couple of times already and it's been successful in this way, let's lean on some of those conventions. If it's brand new, you've created something nobody's ever even seen before, that's a good opportunity to create a brand new brand concept.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:12): I see. I see. So for you guys, what do you feel like, what challenges do your customers face and how are you guys working through those with 'em?", "token_estimate": 528, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:19", "end_timestamp": "06:12", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "c23b6b20-b2de-4d78-8e91-0cbea908e092", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Brian Seales (06:21): Our customers actually, their biggest challenge is making decisions. I think they don't necessarily know exactly what they want or what they don't want or what their audience needs. And if you think about trying to cater to an audience and establish a personality for a company, there's probably 20 different directions that you can go. And not all of them are perfect, and you don't know which one of those is going to be the best one, and you never will. But (06:51): What we do is kind of evaluate the personality of the business and evaluate the target audience and try to come to a middle ground to determine what's going to be best for both of them. I see. And we will explain to our clients why we did the things that we did to help them along. I mean, one of the biggest factors is making sure that whoever is the manager of the organization or the owner of the business is proud of what they're amplifying. And if we're creating a brand for an audience that they don't really like, that's not going to be helpful for anyone. So it is kind of like a feathering off of who's going to benefit more from the branding concept, and then we'll argue with both sides to make sure it's\n\nCole Heilborn (07:33): Right. Sure. But in terms of decisions making is a hard thing for your clients. What benefits do you see after you've created a strong brand? What benefits do your clients end up seeing six months afterwards, a year or five years?\n\nBrian Seales (07:50): Well, I mean, most of the branding concepts that we create are based on a general awareness campaign\n\nCole Heilborn (07:54): Because", "token_estimate": 405, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:21", "end_timestamp": "07:54", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "b3510717-1894-4507-bfb7-cac48fc036fa", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Brian Seales (07:55): It's a new company or it's a rebrand, and we want to make sure that the word gets out. It's not trying to, if it's a product, it's a little bit different. It's branding in a sense, but it's a little bit different. But what it is is they see a quicker return or a growth in their local awareness or regional awareness when their branding consistency is set. If their branding has been hit or miss for the past couple of years, the audience shifts with it and you don't get that full saturation level that you want to get. So by having a consistent awareness campaign for the branding that we've created, that awareness grows, which that means the volume of opportunity for sales grows. We only exist to be an investment to our companies and our clients. And if we're not doing a good job with that, they're not going to, will never hire us to do that. So the whole goal is to encourage a sales return on that, and more awareness means better sales.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:48): Yeah, yeah. No, that makes sense. So when you guys are working with a brand, obviously companies come to you and you don't know anything about them. You can maybe decipher some stuff from their website or their Instagram, but essentially, how do you get to know these companies that come to you? Because essentially you have to become an expert within their field or an expert as to what they do. How do you do that in a quick amount of time without having the history that these companies have when they show up at your door", "token_estimate": 380, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:55", "end_timestamp": "08:48", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}]
